INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q1', 'ap-diag-phys1', 1, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A mass is suspended from the ceiling by one rope at an angle as indicated. The mass is held in place by a second, horizontal rope. Without knowing the exact angle from the ceiling, except that it is greater than 45 degrees, which statement best represents the possible value for tension in the horizontal line?</p>', '["$T$ &gt; <i>mg</i>","$T$ = <i>mg</i>","$T$ &lt; <i>mg</i>","Cannot be determined without knowing the angle"]'::jsonb, '$T$ &lt; <i>mg.</i> Since the mass is stationary, we conclude that the net force is zero. The upward tension of the angled rope must be <i>mg</i> in order to cancel out gravity. The free-body diagram on the hanging mass is:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w35" role="presentation" src="images/f023-01.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">Since the angle θ is greater than 45 degrees, the vertical component of tension $T_{2}$ must be bigger than the horizontal component. (To see this quickly, draw an extreme case, such as 85 degrees.) Therefore, the horizontal component of tension must be less than <i>mg</i>. The horizontal rope $T$ must cancel this horizontal component of $T_{2}$.</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w23" role="presentation" src="images/f023-02.jpg"/></p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q2', 'ap-diag-phys1', 2, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Pushing on a mass of 15 kg with a sideways force of 120 N is not enough to get the mass to start sliding across the floor. Which of the following best describes the coefficient of static friction between the floor and the mass?</p>', '["$\\mu$ = 0.8","$\\mu$ &gt; 0.8","$\\mu$ &lt; 0.8","$\\mu$ = 1.25"]'::jsonb, '$\mu$ &gt; 0.8. The normal force between the mass and the floor is 150 N. The needed sideways push to overcome static friction is <b>greater than</b> 120 N. Dividing these two forces would give us:</p>
<p class="center1">$\mu_s = 120/150 = 0.8$</p>
<p class="exp">Since a greater force is needed, the coefficient of static friction must be greater than this value.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q3', 'ap-diag-phys1', 3, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'While standing on an elevator and experiencing a downward acceleration of 4.9 m/$s^{2}$, what is the force between the floor and your feet? The variable $m$ is your mass.</p>', '["<i>mg</i>","0.5<i>mg</i>","1.5<i>mg</i>","4.9<i>mg</i>"]'::jsonb, '0.5<i>mg</i>. The free-body diagram consists of only two forces.</p> N - mg = ma $$
<p class="exp">Newton’s second law gives us $+N$ − <i>mg</i> = <i>ma</i>. Let $a$ = −0.5$g$, which is half the value of $g$ and downward:</p> N - mg = m(-0.5g) \implies N = 0.5mg $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q4', 'ap-diag-phys1', 4, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'After throwing a ball at an upward angle, the reason the ball continues to go horizontally while falling due to gravity is that</p>', '["the ball’s inertia keeps it going","the force of the throw keeps it going","the energy from the gravitational potential keeps replenishing the lost energy","air pressure keeps the ball from falling too quickly"]'::jsonb, 'The ball’s inertia keeps it going. Newton’s first law says that a body’s inertia maintains its current motion once in motion. After the force of the throw, the only force on the ball is the vertical force of gravity. This causes the ball to accelerate in the vertical direction while the ball’s sideways velocity is maintained due to its inertia.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q5', 'ap-diag-phys1', 5, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Consider the situation in which your hand is pushing a book across a rough desktop with lots of friction. Of the many forces involved, consider only these two individual forces: the force on your hand from the book and the force the book is experiencing from your hand. While the book is accelerating from rest to its final velocity, which statement best compares the force experienced by your hand compared with that experienced by the book?</p>', '["Force on book &gt; force on hand","Force on hand &gt; force on book","Force on hand = force on book","The relationship between these two forces depends on the frictional force."]'::jsonb, 'Force on hand = force on book. Newton’s third law of action-reaction states that the force pair of interaction between two objects is always equal and opposite. The acceleration of either object has no role in the third law. The acceleration comes about from the net force on a single object.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q6', 'ap-diag-phys1', 6, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A puck comes to a stop on a level floor in 20 meters after an initial speed of 10 m/s. What is the coefficient of kinetic friction between the puck and the floor?</p>', '["The mass is required to calculate this answer.","0.25","0.5","0.025"]'::jsonb, '0.25. To find force, we must first find acceleration:</p> a = \frac{v_f - v_i}{t} = \frac{0 - 20}{8} = -2.5\text{ m/s}^2 $$
<p class="exp">The net force horizontally is the friction. There are no other horizontal forces:</p> f_k = \mu_k N  
<p class="exp">Newton’s second law gives us:</p> \Sigma F_x = -f_k = ma  
<p class="exp">Our model for friction gives us:</p> -\mu_k N = ma  
<p class="exp">This value is negative since it is to the left. Since the vertical forces cancel out, $N$ = <i>mg.</i> Substituting for friction from above and <i>mg</i> for $N$:</p> -\mu_k mg = ma  
<p class="exp">Note that mass cancels:</p> \mu_k = \frac{|a|}{g} = \frac{2.5}{10} = 0.25 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q7', 'ap-diag-phys1', 7, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A rock is dropped from an extremely high cliff and experiences free-fall conditions. Which of the following statements is NOT true about the rock’s velocity, acceleration, or displacement between the 3rd and 4th seconds of falling?</p>', '["The rock will fall an additional 4.9 meters.","The rock will speed up by 9.8 m/s.","The rock’s acceleration will remain 9.8 m/$s^{2}$.","The rock’s displacement, velocity, and acceleration vectors are all directed downward."]'::jsonb, 'The rock will fall an additional 4.9 meters is NOT true. An object in free fall will accelerate by a constant 9.8 m/$s^{2}$. Since the rock is already moving downward, this leads to an increase in speed of 9.8 m/s in that 1-second interval. However, the rock will fall much farther than 4.9 m as it is already moving close to 30 m/s downward by the start of the 3rd second. It will fall an additional 35 meters in that 4th second. The rock does indeed fall 4.9 meters in the 1st second of the drop but falls an increasingly greater distance each second thereafter.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q8', 'ap-diag-phys1', 8, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'When using the kinematics equation $x = x_{0} +v$<sub>$x\$0</sub> $t$ + ½ $a$<sub>$x$</sub> $t^{2}$, which of the following is always assumed to be true?</p>', '["<i>a x</i> = zero","$x$ &gt; $x_{0}$","$V$<sub>$x\\$0</sub> is positive.","$a$<sub>$x$</sub> is constant."]'::jsonb, '$a$<sub>$x$</sub> is constant. All the equations of motion are predicated on the assumption of constant acceleration. Any permutation of signs or values will work in any of the equations of motion. If the acceleration is not constant, one must use graphical methods or approximate the acceleration as constant over a narrow interval of time.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q9', 'ap-diag-phys1', 9, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'Consider a projectile launched at 30 degrees above a flat surface. The projectile experiences no air resistance and lands at the same height from which it was launched. Compare the initial and final conditions of the following vector components:</p>
<p class="center1">$V_x \quad V_y \quad a_x \quad a_y$</p>', '["They are all the same at both instances.","They are all different from their initial values.","$V$<sub>$x$</sub> and $V$<sub>$y$</sub> have changed, but $a$<sub>$x$</sub> <i>and a</i><sub>$y$</sub> are constant.","They are all the same except for $V$<sub>$y$</sub> <i>.</i>"]'::jsonb, 'They are all the same except for $V$<sub>$y$</sub>. Projectile motion involves a constant vertical acceleration of −$g$ and no horizontal acceleration at all. Therefore, $a$<sub>$x$</sub> and $a$<sub>$y$</sub> are both constant throughout the flight. Since $a$<sub>$x$</sub> is zero, there is no change in $V$<sub>$x$</sub><i>.</i> So $V$<sub>$x$</sub> is also constant. $V$<sub>$y$</sub><i>,</i> however, is always changing. For a symmetric problem such as this one, the projectile lands with the same vertical speed as it had initially but in the opposite direction so that $V$<sub><i>yf</i></sub> = − $V$<sub><i>yi</i></sub>.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q10', 'ap-diag-phys1', 10, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A physics teacher is swirling a bucket around in a vertical circle. If the bucket slips out of his hand when it is directly overhead, the bucket will</p>', '["fly upward initially","drop straight down","fly out horizontally initially","fly out up and at an angle initially"]'::jsonb, 'The bucket will fly out horizontally initially. Objects executing circular motion have velocity vectors tangent to their motion while experiencing an inward acceleration. If the inward force is released, Newton’s first law dictates that the object follow its velocity vector. Note that gravity will cause the object to fall after it is released, but the initial motion is horizontal.</p> y = \frac{1}{2}gt^2, \quad x = v_x t $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q11', 'ap-diag-phys1', 11, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If the Moon was twice as far from the center of Earth as it is currently, how would its orbital period change?</p>', '["The Moon’s orbital period would double.","The Moon’s orbital period would increase by a factor of 4.","$The Moon’s orbital period would increase by a factor of 2 \\sqrt{2}.$","The Moon’s orbital period would decrease by a factor of 2."]'::jsonb, 'The Moon’s orbital period would increase by a factor of 2 \sqrt{2}. Kepler’s third law of orbital bodies states that their orbital periods squared are proportional to their orbital radius cubed:</p> T^2 \propto R^3 \implies \left(\frac{T_{\text{new}}}{T_{\text{old}}}\right)^2 = \left(\frac{R_{\text{new}}}{R_{\text{old}}}\right)^3 $$
<p class="exp">Substituting $R_{new}$ = 2$R_{old}$:</p> \left(\frac{T_{\text{new}}}{T_{\text{old}}}\right)^2 = 2^3 = 8  
<p class="exp">Cancel $R$ and solve for $T_{new}$:</p> T_{\text{new}} = \sqrt{8}\,T_{\text{old}} = 2\sqrt{2}\,T_{\text{old}}  
<p class="exp">Note that you can also obtain this result by examining the equation for orbital speed from Newton ($v$ = (<i>Gm</i>/$r$)<sup>½</sup>). This shows that the orbital speed of the Moon must decrease by the square root of 2. Coupling this with the knowledge that the new orbit is now twice as large, it will take 2(2<sup>½</sup>) or 2 \sqrt{2} times as long to orbit once around Earth as it used to.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q12', 'ap-diag-phys1', 12, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If you went to another planet, which of the following would be true?</p>', '["Your mass would be the same, but your weight would change.","Your mass would change, but your weight would be the same.","Your mass would be the same, and your weight would be the same.","Your mass would change, and your weight would change."]'::jsonb, 'Your mass would be the same, but your weight would change. Mass is a property of an object and measures the object’s inertia regardless of location. Weight is a force due to gravity and depends both on the object’s mass and on the planetary gravity the object is currently experiencing.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q13', 'ap-diag-phys1', 13, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'You lift a heavy suitcase twice. Each time you lift it to the same height, but the second time you do it in half the time. Compare the work done to and power delivered to the suitcase.</p>', '["Same work, same power","Twice the work, twice the power","Twice the work, same power","Same work, twice the power"]'::jsonb, 'Same work, twice the power.</p>
<p class="center1">Work = force × distance</p>
<p class="exp">For lifting operations, the force is assumed to be equal to the weight of the object for most of the lift. (Briefly, at the beginning of the lift, the lifting force must be greater than the weight in order to get the object moving. This brief force must be different in the second case in order to get the suitcase moving faster. However, this initial force is generally ignored in these calculations as it does not happen over an appreciable distance, just as the decrease in lifting force at the end of the lift is likewise ignored.) The same force over the same distance yields the same work. If the details of the forces contributing to the work are troublesome, you may prefer to think about the energy gained by the suitcase ($W$ = Δ$E$). Since the suitcase has gained the same amount of energy in both cases by being lifted, the work done must likewise be the same:</p>
<p class="center1">Power = work/time</p>
<p class="exp">If the same work is done in half the time, twice the power must be supplied.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q14', 'ap-diag-phys1', 14, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A constant horizontal force of 12 N is applied for 5 meters to move an initially stationary mass of 5 kg. The friction between the floor and the mass does a total of −40 joules of work to the mass. The final speed (in m/s) of the object is</p>', '["2(6)<sup>½</sup>","2(2)<sup>½</sup>","2(10)<sup>½</sup>","4"]'::jsonb, '2(2)<sup>½</sup> m/s. From the work-energy theorem:</p> W_{\text{net}} = \Delta KE = F \cdot d = (10\text{ N})(2\text{ m}) = 20\text{ J} $$
<p class="exp">So the kinetic energy of the mass has gone up by 20 J. Initially, it had no <i>KE</i> since it was stationary. Therefore:</p> KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \implies 20 = \frac{1}{2}(5)v^2 \implies v = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}\text{ m/s} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q15', 'ap-diag-phys1', 15, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A skier begins a downhill run at a height of 9 meters and a speed of 2 m/s. If you ignore friction, what will be her speed when she is 3 meters from the bottom of the slope?</p>', '["6.0 m/s","12.8 m/s","11.1 m/s","7.7 m/s"]'::jsonb, '11.1 m/s. Mechanical energy is conserved since there are no nonconservative forces involved:</p> mgh_i + \frac{1}{2}mv_i^2 = mgh_f + \frac{1}{2}mv_f^2 $$
<p class="exp">Canceling mass in every term and solving for <i>$v_{f}$</i> yields:</p> v_f = \sqrt{v_i^2 + 2g(h_i - h_f)} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q16', 'ap-diag-phys1', 16, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A rubber ball ($m$ = 0.250 kg) strikes a wall horizontally at 3.50 m/s and rebounds elastically. What is the magnitude of impulse delivered to the wall in N ⋅ s?</p>', '["0.875","1.75","2.50","8.75"]'::jsonb, '1.75</p> \Delta p = m(v_f - v_i) = (0.05\text{ kg})(-20 - 15) = -1.75\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s} $$
<p class="exp">Since the collision is elastic, no kinetic energy is lost. Assuming the wall remains stationary, the rubber ball must rebound with the same speed but in the opposite direction:</p> v_f = -v_i  
<p class="exp">Substituting for $v$<sub>$f$</sub> above:</p> J = \Delta p = -1.75\text{ N}\cdot\text{s} \implies |J| = 1.75\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}  
<p class="exp">Here the negative sign indicates that the impulse delivered to the ball is in the opposite direction from the ball’s initial velocity. Note the ball and the wall receive equal and opposite impulses (from Newton’s third law) and the problem asks for magnitude (absolute value).</p>
<p class="expt">Since $F$Δ$t$ is also an impulse, units of N ⋅ s must be the same as kg ⋅ m/s and is quickly shown:</p> 1\text{ N}\cdot\text{s} = 1(\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}^2)\cdot\text{s} = 1\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q17', 'ap-diag-phys1', 17, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A running back ($m$ = 85 kg) running at 1.5 m/s is tackled from the side by another player ($m$ = 75 kg) running perpendicularly to the running back’s original heading at 1.75 m/s. What is the resulting speed of the two entangled players just after the tackle?</p>', '["2.2 m/s","0.25 m/s","1.6 m/s","1.1 m/s"]'::jsonb, '1.1 m/s. Total momentum must be conserved through the collision. So we need to find only the magnitude of the initial momentum of the system. Each player is originally running perpendicularly to the other. Thus, their individual momentums are different components. Imagine one as an $x$-component and the other as a $y$-component. Therefore, the resulting magnitude is found using the Pythagorean theorem. Dividing by total mass yields the speed of the combined players after impact:</p> v_f = \frac{m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{(80)(6) + (100)(-4)}{80 + 100} = \frac{80}{180} = 0.44\text{ m/s} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q18', 'ap-diag-phys1', 18, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'Which of the following ranks the torques applied by the three equal forces <i>A, B,</i> and $C$ to the long thin rod about its fixed axis as indicated above?</p>', '["τ <sub>$C$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$B$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$A$</sub>","τ <sub>$A$</sub> = τ <sub>$B$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$C$</sub>","τ <sub>$B$</sub> = τ <sub>$C$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$A$</sub>","τ <sub>$B$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$A$</sub> &gt; τ <sub>$C$</sub>"]'::jsonb, '$\tau$<sub>$B$</sub> &gt; $\tau$<sub>$A$</sub> &gt; $\tau$ <sub>$C$</sub></p>
<p class="center1">Torque = (force) (distance from axis of rotation) sin θ</p>
<p class="exp">Force $C$ is applied along the lever arm. So force $C$ has an angle of 180°, resulting in no torque.</p> \tau_C = r F \sin(180^\circ) = 0  
<p class="exp">Force $A$ has a much shorter lever arm than force $B$. Therefore, the torque supplied by $A$ is much less than the torque supplied by $B$.</p> \tau_A = r_A F_A \sin(90^\circ) < \tau_B = r_B F_B \sin(90^\circ) $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q19', 'ap-diag-phys1', 19, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A large spherical cloud of dust in deep space that is spinning slowly collapses under its own gravitational force into a spherical cloud 10 times smaller in diameter. How does its rotational speed change?</p>', '["The rotational speed does not change.","The rotational speed increases by a factor of 10.","The rotational speed decreases by a factor of 10.","The rotational speed increases by a factor of 100."]'::jsonb, 'The rotational speed increases by a factor of 100. The rotational inertia ($I$) for a sphere is proportional to $R^{2}$. Because the shape of the cloud is not changing, we can determine that the rotational inertia has decreased by a factor of 10<sup>2</sup>. Since angular momentum is conserved:</p> L_1 = L_2 \implies I_1 \omega_1 = I_2 \omega_2  
<p class="exp">The new angular velocity ($ω_{2}$) must be 100 times bigger:</p> \omega_2 = \frac{I_1}{I_2}\omega_1 = 100\,\omega_1 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q20', 'ap-diag-phys1', 20, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'Two cars approach an intersection at right angles, as pictured above. What is their relative speed to each other?</p>', '["10 mph","45 mph","57 mph","80 mph"]'::jsonb, '57 mph. Relative velocity is the difference in the velocity vectors. When the vectors are subtracted, the resultant is the hypotenuse of a right triangle:</p> |\Delta\vec{v}| = \sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2} = \sqrt{10^2 + 10^2} = 10\sqrt{2}\text{ m/s} \approx 14.1\text{ m/s} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q21', 'ap-diag-phys1', 21, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A container of water (pictured below) has two holes in its side allowing two streams of water to emerge as shown. Initially, the lower hole is twice as deep as the higher hole. Which statement best compares the initial velocities of these two streams of water?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w34" role="presentation" src="images/f020-02.jpg"/></p>', '["They emerge at the same speed.","The lower stream emerges 2½ times as fast as the upper stream.","The lower stream emerges 2 times as fast as the upper stream.","The lower stream emerges 4 times as fast as the upper stream."]'::jsonb, 'According to Bernoulli’s equation, the square of the velocity of fluid relates to the depth of the fluid, assuming the pressure is the same. (This occurs since the streams of water are released into equal atmospheric pressure and is also known as Torricelli’s theorem.) Comparing the surface of the water to one of the streams:</p> P_1 + \rho g h_1 + \frac{1}{2}\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \rho g h_2 + \frac{1}{2}\rho v_2^2 $$
<p class="exp">With $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ being equal and the surface being stationary ($v_{1}$ = 0), we are left with:</p> \rho g h_1 = \rho g h_2 + \frac{1}{2}\rho v_2^2  
<p class="exp">Eliminating and rearranging gives:</p> v_2 = \sqrt{2g(h_1 - h_2)} $$
<p class="exp">Since the lower hole is twice as deep, the quantity in parentheses will be twice as great for the lower hole as compared with the first. To compare velocities, take the square root of both sides.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q22', 'ap-diag-phys1', 22, false, 'C', 'Kinematics', 'medium', 'A car accelerates from rest at 4 m/s^2 for 5 s. Final speed is:', '["10 m/s","15 m/s","20 m/s","25 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'v = u + at = 0 + 4(5) = 20 m/s.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q23', 'ap-diag-phys1', 23, false, 'B', 'Newton''s Laws', 'medium', 'A 5 kg object experiences 20 N net force. Acceleration = ?', '["2 m/s^2","4 m/s^2","100 m/s^2","25 m/s^2"]'::jsonb, 'F = ma => a = F/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s^2.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q24', 'ap-diag-phys1', 24, false, 'B', 'Work-Energy', 'medium', 'A 2 kg ball is lifted 3 m. Gravitational PE gained (g=10 m/s^2):', '["30 J","60 J","6 J","20 J"]'::jsonb, 'PE = mgh = 2 * 10 * 3 = 60 J.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q25', 'ap-diag-phys1', 25, false, 'B', 'Momentum', 'medium', 'A 3 kg object at 4 m/s hits and sticks to a 1 kg stationary object. Combined speed:', '["4 m/s","3 m/s","2 m/s","1 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of momentum: 3(4) = 4*v => v = 3 m/s.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q26', 'ap-diag-phys1', 26, false, 'C', 'Circular Motion', 'medium', 'Centripetal acceleration of speed $v$ in a circle of radius $r$:', '["vr","v/r","v^2/r","r/v^2"]'::jsonb, 'Centripetal acceleration a_c = v^2/r.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q27', 'ap-diag-phys1', 27, false, 'C', 'Waves', 'easy', 'A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. Speed is:', '["2.5 m/s","7 m/s","10 m/s","3 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'v = f*lambda = 5 * 2 = 10 m/s.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q28', 'ap-diag-phys1', 28, false, 'C', 'Rotation', 'hard', 'Disk with I = 2 kg*m^2, omega = 3 rad/s. Rotational KE = ?', '["3 J","6 J","9 J","18 J"]'::jsonb, 'KE = (1/2)*I*omega^2 = (1/2)(2)(9) = 9 J.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q29', 'ap-diag-phys1', 29, false, 'C', 'Oscillations', 'medium', 'A pendulum''s period depends on:', '["Mass of the bob","Amplitude of swing","Length and g only","Air resistance only"]'::jsonb, 'T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g). Period depends only on length L and gravitational acceleration g.'),
  ('ap-diag-phys1_q30', 'ap-diag-phys1', 30, false, 'B', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'Archimedes'' principle: buoyant force equals:', '["Weight of the object","Weight of fluid displaced","Density of the fluid","Pressure at the surface"]'::jsonb, 'The buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid displaced by the submerged object.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q1', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 1, false, 'B', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'According to Bernoulli''s principle, as the speed of a fluid increases, what happens to its internal pressure?', '["Increases","Decreases","Remains constant","Becomes zero"]'::jsonb, 'Faster fluid motion reduces static internal pressure.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q2', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 2, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'Water flows through a horizontal pipe with a constriction. At Point 1, the pipe diameter is $4\text{ cm}$ and fluid speed is $1.0\text{ m/s}$. At Point 2, the diameter narrows to $2\text{ cm}$. What is the fluid speed at Point 2?', '["4.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","0.25 m/s","8.0 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'By the continuity equation for incompressible fluids, $A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$. Since area $A \propto d^2$, $\frac{v_2}{v_1} = \left(\frac{d_1}{d_2}\right)^2 = \left(\frac{4}{2}\right)^2 = 4$. Thus $v_2 = 4(1.0\text{ m/s}) = 4.0\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q3', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 3, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'A solid cube with side length $0.10\text{ m}$ and density $600\text{ kg/m}^3$ floats in water (density $1000\text{ kg/m}^3$). What fraction of the cube''s volume is submerged below the water surface?', '["60%","40%","100%","36%"]'::jsonb, 'For a floating object in static equilibrium, buoyant force equals weight: $F_b = W \implies \rho_{\text{fluid}} V_{\text{sub}} g = \rho_{\text{obj}} V_{\text{total}} g \implies \frac{V_{\text{sub}}}{V_{\text{total}}} = \frac{\rho_{\text{obj}}}{\rho_{\text{fluid}}} = \frac{600}{1000} = 0.60 = 60\%$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q4', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 4, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'A large open water storage tank has a small hole $5.0\text{ m}$ below the water surface. Assuming ideal fluid flow and $g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$, what is the speed of water discharging from the hole?', '["10 m/s","5.0 m/s","7.1 m/s","14 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'Torricelli''s Law (derived from Bernoulli''s equation with $P_1 = P_2 = P_{\text{atm}}$ and $v_1 \approx 0$): $v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10\text{ m/s}^2)(5.0\text{ m})} = \sqrt{100} = 10\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q5', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 5, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'In a hydraulic lift, an input piston of area $0.02\text{ m}^2$ is pushed down with a force of $100\text{ N}$. What force is exerted by the output piston of area $0.50\text{ m}^2$ at the same elevation?', '["2,500 N","500 N","1,000 N","5,000 N"]'::jsonb, 'By Pascal''s Principle, pressure is transmitted undiminished: $P_1 = P_2 \implies \frac{F_1}{A_1} = \frac{F_2}{A_2} \implies F_2 = F_1 \left(\frac{A_2}{A_1}\right) = 100\text{ N} \left(\frac{0.50}{0.02}\right) = 100(25) = 2,500\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q6', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 6, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'What is the absolute hydrostatic pressure at a depth of $20\text{ m}$ below the surface of a lake, assuming atmospheric pressure $P_0 = 1.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$, water density $\rho = 1000\text{ kg/m}^3$, and $g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$?', '["$3.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$2.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$1.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$4.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$"]'::jsonb, 'Absolute pressure $P = P_0 + \rho g h = 1.0 \times 10^5 + (1000)(10)(20) = 1.0 \times 10^5 + 2.0 \times 10^5 = 3.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q7', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 7, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'A heat engine operates between a hot reservoir at $500\text{ K}$ and a cold reservoir at $300\text{ K}$. What is the maximum theoretical (Carnot) thermal efficiency of this engine?', '["40%","60%","20%","67%"]'::jsonb, 'Carnot efficiency $e_C = 1 - \frac{T_C}{T_H} = 1 - \frac{300\text{ K}}{500\text{ K}} = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40 = 40\%$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q8', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 8, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'An ideal gas undergoes an isobaric expansion at constant pressure $P = 2.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$, expanding from $0.010\text{ m}^3$ to $0.030\text{ m}^3$. How much work is done by the gas?', '["4,000 J","2,000 J","6,000 J","8,000 J"]'::jsonb, 'For an isobaric process, work done by the gas is $W = P\Delta V = (2.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa})(0.030 - 0.010\text{ m}^3) = (2.0 \times 10^5)(0.020) = 4,000\text{ J}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q9', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 9, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'In an adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas, $Q = 0$ and the gas does $500\text{ J}$ of work on its surroundings. What is the change in internal energy $\Delta U$ of the gas?', '["-500 J","+500 J","0 J","-1,000 J"]'::jsonb, 'By the First Law of Thermodynamics, $\Delta U = Q - W_{\text{by}}$ (or $\Delta U = Q + W_{\text{on}}$). With $Q = 0$ and work done by the gas $W_{\text{by}} = 500\text{ J}$, $\Delta U = 0 - 500 = -500\text{ J}$. Internal energy decreases.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q10', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 10, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'How does the root-mean-square speed ($v_{\text{rms}}$) of molecules in a monatomic ideal gas change if the absolute temperature in Kelvin is quadrupled?', '["It doubles (increases by a factor of 2)","It quadruples (increases by a factor of 4)","It increases by a factor of 16","It remains unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'From the kinetic theory of gases, $v_{\text{rms}} = \sqrt{\frac{3k_B T}{m}}$. Because $v_{\text{rms}} \propto \sqrt{T}$, quadrupling $T$ ($4T$) scales speed by $\sqrt{4} = 2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q11', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 11, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'According to Bernoulli''s principle, what happens to the internal fluid pressure along a streamline in a horizontal pipe when the fluid speed increases?', '["The pressure decreases","The pressure increases","The pressure remains unchanged","The pressure drops to zero immediately"]'::jsonb, 'Bernoulli''s equation for horizontal flow ($y = \text{const}$) states $P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 = \text{const}$. As velocity $v$ increases, kinetic energy density $\frac{1}{2}\rho v^2$ increases, which requires static pressure $P$ to decrease.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q12', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 12, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'What is true about the net change in internal energy $\Delta U$ of an ideal gas over one complete thermodynamic cycle that returns to its initial state on a PV diagram?', '["$\\Delta U = 0$","$\\Delta U > 0$","$\\Delta U < 0$","$\\Delta U = Q_{\\text{in}}$"]'::jsonb, 'Internal energy $U$ is a state function that depends solely on the current state (temperature) of the ideal gas. After completing a closed cycle, the gas returns to its initial temperature, so $\Delta U = 0$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q13', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 13, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'A container holds $2.0\text{ moles}$ of an ideal gas at $300\text{ K}$ and $1.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$. Using $R = 8.31\text{ J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$, what is the volume of the gas?', '["$0.050\\text{ m}^3$","$0.025\\text{ m}^3$","$0.10\\text{ m}^3$","$0.005\\text{ m}^3$"]'::jsonb, 'From $PV = nRT$: $V = \frac{nRT}{P} = \frac{(2.0\text{ mol})(8.31\text{ J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)})(300\text{ K})}{1.0 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}} = \frac{4,986}{100,000} \approx 0.04986\text{ m}^3 \approx 0.050\text{ m}^3$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q14', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 14, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'During an isochoric (constant volume) heating process, $800\text{ J}$ of heat is transferred to an ideal gas. How much work is done by the gas?', '["0 J","800 J","-800 J","400 J"]'::jsonb, 'Work is $W = \int P\,dV$. In an isochoric process, volume is strictly constant ($\Delta V = 0$), so no boundary work is performed ($W = 0$). All $800\text{ J}$ of heat goes into increasing internal energy.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q15', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 15, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Two metal rods of identical length and cross-sectional area connect a heat source at $100^\circ\text{C}$ to an ice bath at $0^\circ\text{C}$. Rod A has thermal conductivity $k$, and Rod B has thermal conductivity $2k$. What is the ratio of the rate of heat conduction through Rod B compared to Rod A ($\frac{H_B}{H_A}$)?', '["2","4","1/2","1"]'::jsonb, 'Thermal conduction rate is $H = \frac{kA\Delta T}{L}$. With identical $A, L,$ and $\Delta T$, $H \propto k$. Since $k_B = 2k_A$, $\frac{H_B}{H_A} = 2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q16', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 16, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'What does the Second Law of Thermodynamics state regarding the total entropy of an isolated system during an irreversible spontaneous process?', '["The total entropy of the system always increases ($\\Delta S_{\\text{total}} > 0$)","The total entropy remains exactly constant ($\\Delta S_{\\text{total}} = 0$)","The total entropy always decreases to zero","The total entropy fluctuates unpredictably"]'::jsonb, 'The Second Law dictates that spontaneous irreversible processes in an isolated system always result in an increase in total entropy (disorder): $\Delta S_{\text{isolated}} > 0$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q17', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 17, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'A rectangular barge of width $4.0\text{ m}$, length $10.0\text{ m}$, and height $2.0\text{ m}$ floats in freshwater ($\rho = 1000\text{ kg/m}^3$). If the barge sinks $0.50\text{ m}$ deeper when loaded with cargo, what is the mass of the added cargo?', '["20,000 kg","10,000 kg","40,000 kg","80,000 kg"]'::jsonb, 'The added buoyant force supports the added cargo weight: $\Delta F_b = m_{\text{cargo}} g \implies \rho_{\text{water}} \Delta V_{\text{disp}} g = m_{\text{cargo}} g$. The additional volume displaced is $\Delta V = \text{width} \times \text{length} \times \Delta h = (4.0)(10.0)(0.50) = 20.0\text{ m}^3$. Mass $= \rho \Delta V = (1000\text{ kg/m}^3)(20.0\text{ m}^3) = 20,000\text{ kg}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q18', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 18, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'Why does an open glass tube of very narrow radius cause water to climb upward against gravity (capillary action)?', '["Adhesive forces between water and glass molecules are stronger than cohesive forces between water molecules","Cohesive forces between water molecules pull water away from the glass surface","Atmospheric pressure inside the tube is significantly greater than outside","The density of water decreases inside narrow tubes"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary rise occurs when adhesive forces between liquid molecules and the tube wall exceed the cohesive intermolecular forces of the liquid, creating a concave meniscus and drawing liquid upward.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q19', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 19, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'hard', 'A cyclic heat engine executes a clockwise cycle on a $P$-$V$ diagram. The area enclosed by the cycle is $1,200\text{ J}$. If the engine absorbs $4,000\text{ J}$ of heat from the high-temperature reservoir per cycle, what is its actual thermal efficiency?', '["30%","40%","25%","70%"]'::jsonb, 'For a cyclic engine, the net work output $W_{\text{net}}$ equals the area enclosed by the cycle on the $P$-$V$ diagram ($1,200\text{ J}$). Efficiency is $e = \frac{W_{\text{net}}}{Q_H} = \frac{1,200\text{ J}}{4,000\text{ J}} = 0.30 = 30\%$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys2-fluids_q20', 'ap-topic-phys2-fluids', 20, false, 'A', 'Fluids & Thermodynamics', 'medium', 'Which statement correctly summarizes the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics?', '["If systems A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with system C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other, establishing temperature as a well-defined property","Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed between heat and work","The entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero is zero","No heat engine can achieve 100% efficiency"]'::jsonb, 'The Zeroth Law states that thermal equilibrium is a transitive relation, providing the physical foundation for the concept and measurement of temperature via thermometers.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 1, false, 'A', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'easy', 'A vector is given by its components, $A$<sub>$x$</sub> = 2.5 and $A$<sub>$y$</sub> = 7.5. What angle does vector \vec{A} make with the positive $x$-axis?</p>', '["72°","18°","25°","50°"]'::jsonb, 'The angle for the vector to the positive $x$-axis is given by</p> \tan\theta = \frac{A_y}{A_x} = \frac{7.5}{2.5} = 3 \implies \theta = 71.5^\circ \approx 72^\circ $$
<p class="exp">Thus, tan θ = 3 and θ = 71.5° or, rounded off, 72°.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 2, false, 'B', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'easy', 'Which pair of vectors could produce a resultant of 35?</p>', '["15 and 15","20 and 20","30 and 70","20 and 60"]'::jsonb, 'Two vectors have a maximum resultant, whose magnitude is equal to the numerical sum of the vector magnitudes, when the angle between vectors is 0°. The resultant is a minimum at 180°, and the magnitude is equal to the numerical difference between the magnitudes. When this logic is used, only pair (20, 20) produces a maximum and minimum set that could include the given value of 35 if the angle were specified. All the others have maximum and minimum resultants that do not include 35 in their range.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 3, false, 'A', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'easy', 'A vector has a magnitude of 17 units and makes an angle of 20° with the positive $x$-axis. The magnitude of the horizontal component of this vector is</p>', '["16 units","4.1 units","5.8 units","50 units"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for the horizontal component of a vector \vec{A} is $A$<sub>$x$</sub> = |\vec{A} | cos θ, where |\vec{A} | is the magnitude of \vec{A}. When the known values are used, values $A$<sub>$x$</sub> = 17 cos 20 = 15.9 units.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 4, false, 'D', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'medium', 'As the angle between a given vector and the horizontal axis increases from 0° to 90°, the magnitude of the vertical component of this vector</p>', '["decreases","increases and then decreases","decreases and then increases","increases"]'::jsonb, 'The vertical component of a vector is proportional to the sine of the angle, which increases to a maximum at 90°.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 5, false, 'D', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'medium', 'Vector \vec{A} has a magnitude of 10 units and makes an angle of 30° with the horizontal $x$-axis. Vector \vec{B} has a magnitude of 25 units and makes an angle of 50° with the negative $x$-axis. What is the magnitude of the resultant of these two vectors?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w31" role="presentation" src="images/f040-01.jpg"/></p>', '["20","35","15","25"]'::jsonb, 'Form the components of each vector and then add these components, remembering the sign conventions; right and up are positive values. Thus, we have $A$<sub>$x$</sub> = 10 cos 30 = 8.66 and $A$<sub>$y$</sub> = 10 sin 30 = 5, $B$<sub>$x$</sub> = −25 cos 50 = −16.06 and $B$<sub>$y$</sub> = 25 sin 50 = 19.15.</p>
<p class="exp">The components of the resultant are $C$<sub>$x$</sub> = $A$<sub>$x$</sub> $+B$<sub>$x$</sub> = 8.66 − 16.06, so $C$<sub>$x$</sub> = −7.4 units and $C$<sub>$y$</sub> = $A$<sub>$y$</sub> $+B$<sub>$y$</sub> = 5 + 19.15 = 24.15. The magnitude of this resultant vector is given by</p> A_x = A\cos\theta = 10\cos(30^\circ) = 8.66, \quad B_x = -25\cos(50^\circ) = -16.07 $$
<p class="exp">which rounds to |\vec{C}| = 25 units.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 6, false, 'C', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'medium', 'Two concurrent vectors have magnitudes of 3 units and 8 units. The difference between these vectors is 8 units. The angle between these two vectors is</p>', '["34°","56°","79°","113°"]'::jsonb, 'Use the law of cosines where $a$ = 3, $b$ = 8, and $c$ = 8, and solve for cos θ:</p> A_y = A\sin\theta = 10\sin(30^\circ) = 5.0, \quad B_y = 25\sin(50^\circ) = 19.15 $$
<p class="exp">Solving gives cos θ = 0.1875 and θ = 79°.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 7, false, 'C', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'medium', 'Which of the following sets of displacements have equal resultants when performed in the order given?</p>', '["I.6 m east, 9 m north, 12 m west","II.6 m north, 9 m west, 12 m east","III.6 m east, 12 m west, 9 m north","IV.9 m north, 6 m east, 12 m west"]'::jsonb, 'Vectors can be added in any order. The only requirement is that the vectors have the same magnitude and direction as they are shuffled. A look at the four sets of displacements indicates that I, III, and IV consist of the same vectors listed in different orders. These three sets will produce the same resultant.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 8, false, 'C', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'medium', 'Which vector represents the direction of the two concurrent vectors shown below?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w36" role="presentation" src="images/f041-01.jpg"/></p>', '["$ 0^\\circ $","$ 30^\\circ $","$ 45^\\circ $","$ 60^\\circ $"]'::jsonb, 'Sketch the vectors head to tail as if forming a vector triangle in construction. The resultant is drawn from the tail of the first vector to the head of the second. Choose the horizontal vector as the first one; then choice (C) is the general direction of the resultant.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 9, false, 'B', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'hard', 'Three forces act concurrently on a point $P$ as shown below. Which vector represents the direction of the resultant force on point $P$?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w35" role="presentation" src="images/f041-02.jpg"/></p>', '["$ 0^\\circ $","$ 30^\\circ $","$ 60^\\circ $","$ 90^\\circ $"]'::jsonb, 'Sketch the vectors in any order, as shown below. Draw the resultant from the tail of the first vector to the head of the last.</p> R = \sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2} = \sqrt{(-7.41)^2 + (24.15)^2} \approx 25.3 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 10, false, 'B', 'Vectors & Trigonometry', 'hard', 'On a baseball field, first base is about 30 m away from home plate. A batter gets a hit and runs toward first base. She runs 3 m past the base and then runs back to stand on it. The magnitude of her final displacement from home plate is</p>', '["27 m","30 m","33 m","36 m"]'::jsonb, 'The displacement is in the direction of first base and is equal in magnitude to the straight-line distance between home plate and first base (30 m).</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 11, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'medium', 'Vector $\vec{A}$ has magnitude $10\text{ N}$ directed along the $+x$-axis. Vector $\vec{B}$ has magnitude $10\text{ N}$ directed at $120^\circ$ counterclockwise from the $+x$-axis. What is the magnitude of $\vec{R} = \vec{A} + \vec{B}$?', '["$10\\text{ N}$","$14.1\\text{ N}$","$0\\text{ N}$","$20\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '$R_x = 10 + 10\cos 120^\circ = 10 - 5 = 5\text{ N}$. $R_y = 0 + 10\sin 120^\circ = 5\sqrt{3}\text{ N}$. $|\vec{R}| = \sqrt{5^2 + (5\sqrt{3})^2} = \sqrt{25 + 75} = \sqrt{100} = 10\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 12, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'easy', 'A boat aims directly across a river flowing east at $3.0\text{ m/s}$. The boat''s speed relative to the water is $4.0\text{ m/s}$ due north. What is the boat''s resultant velocity relative to the riverbank?', '["$5.0\\text{ m/s}$ at $53^\\circ$ east of north","$5.0\\text{ m/s}$ at $37^\\circ$ east of north","$7.0\\text{ m/s}$ due northeast","$1.0\\text{ m/s}$ due north"]'::jsonb, '$v = \sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2} = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.0\text{ m/s}$. The direction is $\theta = \arctan(3/4) \approx 36.9^\circ \approx 37^\circ$ east of north.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 13, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'easy', 'If the dot product of two non-zero vectors $\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = 0$, what can be concluded about their orientation?', '["They are parallel and point in the same direction","They are anti-parallel","They are mutually perpendicular (orthogonal)","They have equal magnitudes"]'::jsonb, '$\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = |A||B|\cos\theta$. If $|A|, |B| \neq 0$ and dot product is zero, $\cos\theta = 0 \implies \theta = 90^\circ$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 14, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'medium', 'Three coplanar forces act on an object at rest: $\vec{F}_1 = 6\hat{i} - 2\hat{j}$, $\vec{F}_2 = -2\hat{i} + 8\hat{j}$ (in Newtons). What third force $\vec{F}_3$ will maintain static equilibrium?', '["$+4\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j}\\text{ N}$","$-8\\hat{i} - 10\\hat{j}\\text{ N}$","$+8\\hat{i} + 10\\hat{j}\\text{ N}$","$-4\\hat{i} - 6\\hat{j}\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '$\vec{F}_1 + \vec{F}_2 + \vec{F}_3 = \vec{0} \implies (6-2)\hat{i} + (-2+8)\hat{j} + \vec{F}_3 = \vec{0} \implies 4\hat{i} + 6\hat{j} + \vec{F}_3 = \vec{0} \implies \vec{F}_3 = -4\hat{i} - 6\hat{j}\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 15, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'easy', 'A vector $\vec{V}$ has components $V_x = -8.0\text{ m/s}$ and $V_y = +6.0\text{ m/s}$. In which quadrant does $\vec{V}$ point, and what is its magnitude?', '["Quadrant II, magnitude $10.0\\text{ m/s}$","Quadrant I, magnitude $10.0\\text{ m/s}$","Quadrant III, magnitude $14.0\\text{ m/s}$","Quadrant IV, magnitude $7.0\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 'With $V_x < 0$ and $V_y > 0$, the vector lies in Quadrant II. Magnitude $= \sqrt{(-8)^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{64 + 36} = 10.0\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 16, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'hard', 'Two vectors $\vec{P}$ and $\vec{Q}$ have equal magnitudes $M$. If the magnitude of their vector sum $|\vec{P} + \vec{Q}| = M$, what is the angle between them?', '["$60^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$180^\\circ$"]'::jsonb, '$|\vec{P}+\vec{Q}|^2 = M^2 + M^2 + 2M^2\cos\theta = M^2 \implies 2M^2(1 + \cos\theta) = M^2 \implies 1 + \cos\theta = 1/2 \implies \cos\theta = -1/2 \implies \theta = 120^\circ$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 17, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'medium', 'A hiker walks $3.0\text{ km}$ north, then $4.0\text{ km}$ east, and finally $6.0\text{ km}$ south. What is the straight-line displacement from the starting point?', '["$5.0\\text{ km}$ northeast","$13.0\\text{ km}$ southeast","$5.0\\text{ km}$ southeast ($37^\\circ$ south of east)","$7.0\\text{ km}$ south"]'::jsonb, 'Net east-west: $+4.0\text{ km}$. Net north-south: $3.0 - 6.0 = -3.0\text{ km}$. $d = \sqrt{4^2 + (-3)^2} = 5.0\text{ km}$. Angle: $\arctan(3/4) \approx 37^\circ$ south of east.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 18, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'hard', 'What is the cross product magnitude $|\vec{A} \times \vec{B}|$ if $|\vec{A}| = 5\text{ m}$, $|\vec{B}| = 4\text{ m}$, and their dot product $\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = 12\text{ m}^2$?', '["$8\\text{ m}^2$","$20\\text{ m}^2$","$12\\text{ m}^2$","$16\\text{ m}^2$"]'::jsonb, '$\cos\theta = \frac{12}{5 \times 4} = \frac{12}{20} = 0.60$. Since $\sin\theta = \sqrt{1 - 0.60^2} = 0.80$, $|\vec{A} \times \vec{B}| = AB\sin\theta = 20 \times 0.80 = 16\text{ m}^2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 19, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'easy', 'A unit vector in the direction of vector $\vec{C} = 3\hat{i} - 4\hat{j}$ is given by:', '["$0.6\\hat{i} - 0.8\\hat{j}$","$3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j}$","$0.8\\hat{i} - 0.6\\hat{j}$","$\\frac{1}{7}(3\\hat{i} - 4\\hat{j})$"]'::jsonb, 'Magnitude of $\vec{C} = \sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = 5$. Unit vector $\hat{u} = \vec{C}/|C| = (3/5)\hat{i} - (4/5)\hat{j} = 0.6\hat{i} - 0.8\hat{j}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-vectors_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 20, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-vectors', 'medium', 'A plane flies due north with airspeed $200\text{ km/h}$. A crosswind blows from the west toward the east at $50\text{ km/h}$. What heading must the pilot steer to maintain a true north ground track?', '["Approximately $14.5^\\circ$ east of north","Approximately $14.5^\\circ$ west of north","Approximately $25.0^\\circ$ west of north","Due north without compensation"]'::jsonb, 'To cancel the wind''s eastward component ($50\text{ km/h}$), the plane''s airspeed component must satisfy $200\sin\phi = 50 \implies \sin\phi = 50/200 = 0.25 \implies \phi = \arcsin(0.25) \approx 14.5^\circ$ west of north.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 1, false, 'D', 'Energy & Work', 'easy', 'Which of the following are the units for the spring constant, <span>$k$</span>?</p>', '["kg · $m^{2}$/$s^{2}$","kg · $s^{2}$","kg · m/s","kg/$s^{2}$"]'::jsonb, 'The units for the spring or force constant are provided by Hooke’s law, $F$ = <i>kx</i>, and are newtons per meter. Recall that 1 N = 1 kg m/$s^{2}$, so dividing by $m$ gives kg/$s^{2}$.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 2, false, 'D', 'Energy & Work', 'easy', 'Which of the following is an expression for mechanical power?</p>', '["<i>Ft</i>/$m$","$F$ <sup>2</sup>$m$/$a$","<i>Fm</i><sup>2</sup>/$t$","$F$ <sup>2</sup>$t$/$m$"]'::jsonb, 'Power is equal to work done divided by time. Therefore,</p>
<p class="center1">$P = \frac{W}{t} = \frac{Fd}{t}$</p>
<p class="exp">Using some algebra and kinematics, we see that</p>
<p class="center1">$P = \frac{Fd}{t} = Fv = Fat = F\left(\frac{F}{m}\right)t = \frac{F^2 t}{m}$</p>
<p class="exp">You could also get the answer by verifying which expression has units of joules per second.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 3, false, 'A', 'Energy & Work', 'easy', 'A pendulum consisting of a mass $m$ attached to a light string of length <i>ℓ</i> is displaced from its rest position, making an angle θ with the vertical. It is then released and allowed to swing freely. Which of the following expressions represents the velocity of the mass when it reaches its lowest position?</p>', '["$ 0.5\\text{ J} $","$ 1.0\\text{ J} $","$ 2.0\\text{ J} $","$ 4.0\\text{ J} $"]'::jsonb, 'Consider the sketch below of the situation:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w55" role="presentation" src="images/f104-03.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">From the geometry of the sketch, note that</p>
<p class="center1">$h = \ell - \ell\cos\theta = \ell(1 - \cos\theta)$</p>
<p class="exp">If it is assumed that there is no friction, gravity is the only conservative force acting to do work. Therefore,</p>
<p class="center1">$ME_{\text{bottom}} = ME_{\text{top}}, KE_{\text{bottom}} = PE_{\text{top}}, \frac{1}{2}m v^2 = mgh$</p>
<p class="exp">and so, at the bottom,</p>
<p class="center1">$v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2g\ell(1 - \cos\theta)}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 4, false, 'C', 'Energy & Work', 'medium', 'An engine maintains constant power on a conveyor belt machine. If the belt’s velocity is doubled, the magnitude of its average acceleration</p>', '["is doubled","is quartered","is halved","is quadrupled"]'::jsonb, 'Power is equal to the product of the average force applied times the velocity. If the velocity is doubled, and the power is constant, the average force must be halved. Since $F$ = <i>ma</i>, the average acceleration of the belt must be halved as well.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 5, false, 'B', 'Energy & Work', 'medium', 'A mass $m$ is moving horizontally along a nearly frictionless floor with velocity $v$. The mass now encounters a part of the floor that has a coefficient of kinetic friction given by $\mu$. The total distance traveled by the mass before it is slowed by friction to a stop is given by</p>', '["2$v^{2}$/<i>μg</i>","$v^{2}$/2<i>μg</i>","2<i>μgv</i><sup>2</sup>","<i>μv</i><sup>2</sup>/2$g$"]'::jsonb, 'The only applied force is friction, which is doing work to stop the mass. This work is being taken from the initial kinetic energy. For friction, we know that $f$ = <i>μN</i>, and since the motion is horizontal, $N$ = <i>mg</i>. Let $x$ be the distance traveled while stopping; therefore, we can write that $W$<sub>$f$</sub> = <i>KE</i> and:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w21" role="presentation" src="images/f104-05a.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">Solving for $x$, we get:</p>
<p class="center1">$x = \frac{v^2}{2\mu g}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 6, false, 'D', 'Energy & Work', 'medium', 'Two unequal masses are dropped simultaneously from the same height. The two masses will experience the same change in</p>', '["acceleration","kinetic energy","potential energy","velocity"]'::jsonb, 'Objects dropped simultaneously from the same height have the same constant acceleration, which is the change in velocity. The unequal masses will provide for different energies, but the velocity changes will be the same.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 7, false, 'B', 'Energy & Work', 'medium', 'A pendulum that consists of a 2-kg mass swings to a maximum vertical displacement of 17 cm above its rest position. At its lowest point, the kinetic energy of the mass is equal to</p>', '["0.33 J","3.33 J","33.3 J","333 J"]'::jsonb, 'Since we have a conservative system,</p>
<p class="center1">$KE_{\text{bottom}} = PE_{\text{top}} = mgh = (2)(9.8)(0.17)$</p>
<p class="exp">Remember to change 17 cm to 0.17 m!</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 8, false, 'C', 'Energy & Work', 'medium', 'A 0.3-kg mass rests on top of a spring that has been compressed by 0.04 m. Neglect any frictional effects, and consider the spring to be massless. Then, if the spring has a constant $k$ equal to 2,000 N/m, to what height will the mass rise when the system is released?</p>', '["1.24 m","0.75 m","0.54 m","1.04 m"]'::jsonb, 'We are dealing with a conservative system, so the initial starting energy is just the potential energy of the compressed spring. This energy supplies the work needed to raise the mass a height $h$, which is a gain in gravitational potential energy. Thus, we equate these two expressions and solve for the height:</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{2}(2,000)(0.4)^2 = (0.3)(9.8)h$</p>
<p class="exp">Thus, $h$ = 0.54 m.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 9, false, 'D', 'Energy & Work', 'hard', 'A box is pulled along a smooth floor by a force $F$, making an angle θ with the horizontal. As θ increases, the amount of work done to pull the box the same distance, <span>$d$</span>,</p>', '["increases","increases and then decreases","remains the same","decreases"]'::jsonb, 'The component of the applied force in the horizontal direction depends on the cosine of the angle. This value decreases with increasing angle. Thus, the work decreases as well.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 10, false, 'C', 'Energy & Work', 'hard', 'As the time needed to run up a flight of stairs decreases, the amount of work done against gravity</p>', '["increases","decreases","remains the same","increases and then decreases"]'::jsonb, 'The work done is independent of the time or path taken because gravity is a conservative force. The power generated is affected by time, but the work done to run up the stairs remains the same as long as the same mass is raised to the same height.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 11, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'medium', 'A spring with force constant $k = 200\text{ N/m}$ is compressed by $0.10\text{ m}$ and used to launch a $0.25\text{ kg}$ ball horizontally across a frictionless table. What is the launch speed of the ball?', '["$2.8\\text{ m/s}$","$4.0\\text{ m/s}$","$1.4\\text{ m/s}$","$8.0\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of energy: $\frac{1}{2}kx^2 = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \implies v = x\sqrt{k/m} = 0.10\sqrt{200/0.25} = 0.10\sqrt{800} = 0.10(28.28) \approx 2.83\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 12, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'medium', 'A conservative force acting on a particle is given by $F(x) = -4x^3$ (in Newtons). What is the potential energy function $U(x)$, assuming $U(0) = 0$?', '["$U(x) = -x^4$","$U(x) = x^4$","$U(x) = 12x^2$","$U(x) = -12x^2$"]'::jsonb, '$U(x) = -\int_0^x F(x'')dx'' = -\int_0^x (-4x''^3)dx'' = [x''^4]_0^x = x^4$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 13, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'hard', 'A roller coaster car of mass $m$ starts from rest at height $h$ and descends along a frictionless track into a vertical loop of radius $R$. What is the minimum height $h$ required for the car to maintain contact with the track at the top of the loop?', '["$h = 2.0R$","$h = 3.0R$","$h = 2.5R$","$h = 1.5R$"]'::jsonb, 'At the top of the loop, minimum speed to avoid losing contact satisfies $mg = mv^2/R \implies v^2 = gR$. Conservation of energy from height $h$: $mgh = mg(2R) + \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = 2mgR + \frac{1}{2}mgR = 2.5mgR \implies h = 2.5R$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 14, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'easy', 'A constant force $F = 50\text{ N}$ pulls a sled a distance $d = 10\text{ m}$ across snow at an angle of $60^\circ$ above horizontal. How much work is done by this force?', '["$500\\text{ J}$","$433\\text{ J}$","$125\\text{ J}$","$250\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '$W = F d \cos\theta = 50 \times 10 \times \cos 60^\circ = 500 \times 0.50 = 250\text{ J}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 15, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'medium', 'An electric motor delivers $2.0\text{ kW}$ of mechanical power to hoist a $400\text{ kg}$ crate at constant speed. What is the upward speed of the crate ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$0.50\\text{ m/s}$","$2.0\\text{ m/s}$","$5.0\\text{ m/s}$","$0.20\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '$P = F v = mg v \implies v = \frac{P}{mg} = \frac{2000\text{ W}}{400 \times 10\text{ N}} = \frac{2000}{4000} = 0.50\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 16, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'easy', 'A block of mass $m$ slides across a rough horizontal patch of length $L$ with coefficient of kinetic friction $\mu_k$. How much mechanical energy is dissipated as thermal energy?', '["$\\frac{1}{2}\\mu_k mg L$","$\\mu_k mg L$","$2\\mu_k mg L$","$\\mu_k m g / L$"]'::jsonb, 'Thermal energy generated equals the work done by friction: $W_{nc} = -f_k L = -(\mu_k mg)L$. The dissipated thermal energy is $\Delta E_{th} = \mu_k mg L$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 17, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'medium', 'A pendulum bob of mass $m$ is released from rest at an angle $\theta_0$ with the vertical. What is its speed at the lowest point of the swing if the string has length $L$?', '["$\\sqrt{2gL\\cos\\theta_0}$","$\\sqrt{gL(1 - \\sin\\theta_0)}$","$\\sqrt{2gL(1 - \\cos\\theta_0)}$","$\\sqrt{2gL(1 + \\cos\\theta_0)}$"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical drop: $\Delta h = L - L\cos\theta_0 = L(1 - \cos\theta_0)$. By conservation of energy: $\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = mg\Delta h \implies v = \sqrt{2gL(1 - \cos\theta_0)}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 18, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'medium', 'A graph shows force $F(x)$ vs position $x$ as a trapezoid from $x = 0$ to $x = 6\text{ m}$, with parallel bases of lengths $6\text{ m}$ and $2\text{ m}$, and maximum height $10\text{ N}$. What is the total work done?', '["$60\\text{ J}$","$30\\text{ J}$","$80\\text{ J}$","$40\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, 'Work is the area under $F-x$: $\text{Area} = \frac{b_1 + b_2}{2}h = \frac{6 + 2}{2}(10) = 4 \times 10 = 40\text{ J}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 19, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'easy', 'If the kinetic energy of an object is quadrupled, by what factor does its linear momentum increase?', '["$2$","$4$","$\\sqrt{2}$","$16$"]'::jsonb, '$K = \frac{p^2}{2m} \implies p = \sqrt{2mK}$. If $K$ is multiplied by 4, momentum $p$ increases by $\sqrt{4} = 2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-energy_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 20, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-energy', 'easy', 'A force is non-conservative if:', '["The work done depends solely on the initial and final positions","The total work done on a closed loop is non-zero and depends on the path taken","It can be derived from the negative gradient of a scalar potential energy","Mechanical energy of the system is strictly conserved"]'::jsonb, 'Non-conservative forces (like friction and air resistance) dissipate mechanical energy into heat/sound; the work done along a closed path is non-zero and path-dependent.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 1, false, 'D', 'Gravitation', 'easy', 'What is the value of $g$ at a height above Earth’s surface that is equal to the radius of Earth?</p>', '["9.8 N/kg","4.9 N/kg","6.93 N/kg","2.45 N/kg"]'::jsonb, 'The value of $g$ varies inversely with the square of the distance from the center of Earth; therefore, if we double the distance from the center (as in this case), the value of $g$ decreases by one-fourth: 1/4(9.8) = 2.45. Since $g = F$/$m$, alternative units are newtons per kilogram.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 2, false, 'A', 'Gravitation', 'easy', 'A planet has half the mass of Earth and half the radius. Compared to the acceleration due to gravity near the surface of Earth, the acceleration of gravity near the surface of this other planet is</p>', '["twice as much","one-fourth as much","half as much","the same"]'::jsonb, 'If, using the formula for $g$, we take half the mass, the value decreases by one-half. If we decrease the radius by half, the value will increase by four times. Combining both effects results in an overall increase of two times.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 3, false, 'D', 'Gravitation', 'easy', 'Which of the following is an expression for the acceleration of gravity at the surface of a planet with uniform density ρ and radius <span>$r$</span>?</p>', '["$G$(4πρ/3$r^{2}$)","$G$(4πρ$r^{2}$/3)","$G$(4πρ/3$r$)","$G$(4π$r$ρ/3)"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for $g$ is $g$ = <i>GM</i>/$r^{2}$. The planet is essentially a sphere of mass $M$, radius $r$, and density ρ (with $M = V$ρ, where $V$ is the volume). The volume of the planet is given by $V$ = 4/3<i>πr</i><sup>3</sup>. Making the substitutions yields:</p> g = \frac{GM}{r^2} \implies g'' = \frac{GM}{(2R)^2} = \frac{g}{4} = \frac{9.8}{4} = 2.45\text{ N/kg} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 4, false, 'C', 'Gravitation', 'medium', 'What is the orbital velocity of a satellite at a height of 300 km above the surface of Earth? (The mass of Earth is approximately 6 × 10<sup>24</sup> kg, and its radius is 6.4 × 10<span><sup>6</sup></span> m.)</p>', '["5.42 × 10<sup>1</sup> m/s","1.15 × 10<sup>6</sup> m/s","7.7 × 10<sup>3</sup> m/s","6 × 10<sup>6</sup> m/s"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for orbital velocity is</p> v_{\text{orbit}} = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} $$
<p class="nump">where $r$ is the distance from the center of Earth. In this case, we must add 300 km = 300,000 m to the radius of Earth. Thus, $r$ = 6.7 × 10<sup>6</sup> m. Substituting the given values yields $v_{orbit}$ = 7,728 or 7.7 × 10<sup>3</sup> m/s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 5, false, 'B', 'Gravitation', 'medium', 'What is the escape velocity from the Moon, given that the mass of the Moon is 7.2 × 10<sup>22</sup> kg and its radius is 1.778 <span>×</span> 10<span><sup>6</sup></span> m?</p>', '["1.64 × 10<sup>3</sup> m/s","2.32 × 10<sup>3</sup> m/s","2.69 × 10<sup>6</sup> m/s","5.38 × 10<sup>6</sup> m/s"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for escape velocity is</p> T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}} $$
<p class="nump">Substituting the given values yields $v_{esc}$ = 2,324 or 2.32 × 10<sup>3</sup> m/s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 6, false, 'A', 'Gravitation', 'medium', 'A black hole theoretically has an escape velocity that is greater than or equal to the velocity of light (3 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s). If the effective mass of the black hole is equal to the mass of the Sun (2 × 10<sup>30</sup> kg), what is the effective “radius” (called the “Schwarzschild radius”) of the black hole?</p>', '["3 × 10<sup>3</sup> m","1.5 × 10<sup>3</sup> m","8.9 × 10<sup>6</sup> m","4.45 × 10<sup>6</sup> m"]'::jsonb, 'From question 6, we know that the escape velocity is given by $ g = G\frac{M}{R^2} $ To find $r$, we need to square both sides, and then solve for the radius. This yields $r$ = 2<i>GM</i>/$v^{2}$. Substituting the given values yields $r$ = 3,000 or 3 × 10<sup>3</sup> m. (This is only a “theoretical” size for the black hole. As an interesting exercise, try calculating the value of $g$ on such an object!)</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 7, false, 'C', 'Gravitation', 'medium', 'What is the gravitational force of attraction between two trucks, each of mass 20,000 kg, separated by a distance of 2 m?</p>', '["0.057 N","0.013 N","0.0067 N","1.20 N"]'::jsonb, 'We use the formula for gravitational force:</p>
<p class="center1">$F = \frac{GM_1 M_2}{r^2}$</p>
<p class="nump">Substituting the given values (don’t forget to square the distance!) yields $F$ = 0.0067 N.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 8, false, 'D', 'Gravitation', 'medium', 'The gravitational force between two masses is 36 N. If the distance between masses is tripled, the force of gravity will be</p>', '["the same","18 N","9 N","4 N"]'::jsonb, 'The force of gravity is an inverse-square-law relationship. This means that, as the distance is tripled, the force is decreased by one-ninth. One-ninth of 36 N is 4 N.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 9, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'easy', 'Two spherical satellites of mass $M$ orbit each other separated by distance $R$. If their separation is doubled to $2R$, how does the gravitational attraction between them change?', '["It decreases by a factor of 4","It decreases by a factor of 2","It remains unchanged","It decreases by a factor of 8"]'::jsonb, 'Newton''s law of universal gravitation: $F_g = \frac{GM_1M_2}{R^2}$. If distance doubles, force scales as $1/2^2 = 1/4$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 10, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'A satellite orbits Earth in a stable circular orbit of radius $r$ with orbital speed $v_0$. What is the orbital speed of a satellite in a circular orbit of radius $4r$?', '["$0.25 v_0$","$0.50 v_0$","$2.0 v_0$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} v_0$"]'::jsonb, 'Centripetal force is gravity: $\frac{mv^2}{r} = \frac{GMm}{r^2} \implies v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$. At radius $4r$, $v'' = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{4r}} = \frac{1}{2}v_0$.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 11, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'easy', 'What is Kepler''s Third Law relationship between orbital period $T$ and semi-major axis $a$ for planets orbiting the Sun?', '["$T^3 \\propto a^2$","$T \\propto a^2$","$T^2 \\propto a^3$","$T^2 \\propto a$"]'::jsonb, 'Kepler''s third law states that the square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis: $T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2}{GM}a^3$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 12, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'An astronaut weighs $600\text{ N}$ on Earth''s surface. What is the astronaut''s weight in an orbit at an altitude equal to Earth''s radius $R_E$ above the surface?', '["$300\\text{ N}$","$0\\text{ N}$","$75\\text{ N}$","$150\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, 'At altitude $R_E$, the distance from Earth''s center is $r = R_E + R_E = 2R_E$. Weight scales as $1/r^2 = 1/2^2 = 1/4$. $W = 600 / 4 = 150\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 13, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'easy', 'The escape velocity from the surface of a spherical planet of mass $M$ and radius $R$ is given by:', '["$v_{esc} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2GM}{R}}$","$v_{esc} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{R}}$","$v_{esc} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{2R}}$","$v_{esc} = \\dfrac{2GM}{R^2}$"]'::jsonb, 'Setting total mechanical energy equal to zero at infinity: $\frac{1}{2}mv_{esc}^2 - \frac{GMm}{R} = 0 \implies v_{esc} = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 14, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'Planet X has twice the mass of Earth and twice the radius of Earth. How does the gravitational acceleration $g_X$ at Planet X''s surface compare to Earth''s surface gravity $g$?', '["$g_X = 1.0 g$","$g_X = 0.50 g$","$g_X = 2.0 g$","$g_X = 0.25 g$"]'::jsonb, '$g = \frac{GM}{R^2}$. For Planet X: $g_X = \frac{G(2M)}{(2R)^2} = \frac{2}{4}\frac{GM}{R^2} = 0.50 g$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 15, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'easy', 'In an elliptical orbit, at which point is the orbiting body traveling at its maximum orbital speed?', '["Apoapsis (farthest distance)","At the semi-minor axis intersection","Periapsis (closest approach)","Speed is constant at all points"]'::jsonb, 'By conservation of angular momentum ($L = m r v_\perp = \text{const}$), speed is greatest when distance $r$ is smallest (periapsis).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 16, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'What is the total mechanical energy $E$ of a satellite of mass $m$ in a bound circular orbit of radius $r$ around a planet of mass $M$?', '["$E = +\\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$","$E = -\\dfrac{GMm}{r}$","$E = 0$","$E = -\\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$"]'::jsonb, '$K = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \frac{GMm}{2r}$ and $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$. Total energy $E = K + U = \frac{GMm}{2r} - \frac{GMm}{r} = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 17, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'If Earth were to magically shrink to half its current radius while maintaining its total mass, how would the orbital period of the Moon change?', '["It would remain completely unchanged","It would decrease by half","It would double","It would decrease by a factor of 4"]'::jsonb, 'The gravitational force on the Moon depends only on the Earth''s mass and the center-to-center separation. Since neither changes, the Moon''s orbit and period are unaffected.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 18, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'medium', 'According to Kepler''s Second Law, the line connecting a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time. This law is a direct consequence of:', '["Conservation of linear momentum","Conservation of angular momentum under a central force","Newton''s Third Law","Conservation of electric charge"]'::jsonb, 'Because gravitational force is radial (central), the torque $\vec{\tau} = \vec{r} \times \vec{F} = \vec{0}$, so orbital angular momentum $\vec{L}$ is strictly conserved ($dA/dt = L/(2m) = \text{const}$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 19, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'easy', 'A geosynchronous satellite has an orbital period equal to Earth''s rotational period (24 hours). What determines its required orbital altitude?', '["The mass and solar panel area of the satellite","The launch vehicle thrust capacity","Kepler''s Third Law based on Earth''s mass and rotational period","Atmospheric density fluctuations"]'::jsonb, 'Setting $T = 86400\text{ s}$ in $T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2}{GM}r^3$ uniquely fixes the semi-major axis $r \approx 42,164\text{ km}$ regardless of satellite mass.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-gravitation_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 20, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-gravitation', 'hard', 'The gravitational field inside a uniform spherical shell of mass $M$ and radius $R$ at any point $r < R$ is:', '["$\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$ pointing toward the center","Constant and equal to $\\dfrac{GM}{R^2}$","Linearly increasing with radius $r$","Zero everywhere inside the shell"]'::jsonb, 'Newton''s shell theorem proves that the gravitational forces from all symmetric mass elements of a uniform spherical shell cancel identically at any interior point.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 1, false, 'D', 'Linear Momentum', 'easy', 'Which of the following expressions, where $p$ represents the linear momentum of the particle, is equivalent to the kinetic energy of a moving particle?</p>', '["<i>mp</i><sup>2</sup>","$m^{2}$/2$p$","2$p$/$m$","$p^{2}$/2$m$"]'::jsonb, 'If we multiply the formula for kinetic energy by the ratio $m$/$m$, we see that the formula for kinetic energy becomes</p>
<p class="center1">$KE = \left(\frac{1}{2m}\right)(m^2)(v^2) = \frac{p^2}{2m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 2, false, 'A', 'Linear Momentum', 'easy', 'Two carts having masses 1.5 kg and 0.7 kg, respectively, are initially at rest and are held together by a compressed massless spring. When released, the 1.5-kg cart moves to the left with a velocity of 7 m/s. What is the velocity and direction of the 0.7-kg cart?</p>', '["15 m/s right","15 m/s left","7 m/s left","7 m/s right"]'::jsonb, 'Momentum is conserved, so (1.5)(7) = 0.7$v$. Thus, $v$ = 15 m/s. The direction is to the right since in a recoil the masses go in opposite directions.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 3, false, 'C', 'Linear Momentum', 'easy', 'The product of an object’s instantaneous momentum and its acceleration is equal to its</p>', '["applied force","kinetic energy","power output","net force"]'::jsonb, 'An object’s instantaneous momentum times its acceleration will equal its power output in units of joules per second or watts.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 4, false, 'B', 'Linear Momentum', 'medium', 'A ball with a mass of 0.15 kg has a velocity of 5 m/s. It strikes a wall perpendicularly and bounces off straight back with a velocity of 3 m/s. The ball underwent a change in momentum equal to</p>', '["0.30 kg · m/s","1.20 kg · m/s","0.15 kg · m/s","5 kg · m/s"]'::jsonb, 'The change in momentum is a vector quantity. The rebound velocity is in the opposite direction, so Δ$v$ = 5 − (−3) = 8 m/s. The change in momentum is</p>
<p class="center1">$\Delta p = (0.15)(8) = 1.20\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 5, false, 'C', 'Linear Momentum', 'medium', 'What braking force is supplied to a 3,000-kg car traveling with a velocity of 35 m/s that is stopped in 12 s?</p>', '["29,400 N","3,000 N","8,750 N","105,000 N"]'::jsonb, 'The formula is $F$Δ$t = m$Δ$v$. Solving for the force, we get</p>
<p class="center1">$F = \frac{(3,000)(35)}{12} = 8,750\text{ N}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 6, false, 'D', 'Linear Momentum', 'medium', 'A 0.1-kg baseball is thrown with a velocity of 35 m/s. The batter hits it straight back with a velocity of 60 m/s. What is the magnitude of the average impulse exerted on the ball by the bat?</p>', '["3.5 N · s","2.5 N · s","7.5 N · s","9.5 N · s"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse is equal to the change in momentum, which is</p>
<p class="center1">$\Delta p = |0.1[-60 - 35]| = 9.5\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}$</p>
<p class="nump">because of the change in direction of the ball.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 7, false, 'D', 'Linear Momentum', 'medium', 'A 1-kg object is moving with a velocity of 6 m/s to the right. It collides and sticks to a 2-kg object moving with a velocity of 3 m/s in the same direction. How much kinetic energy was lost in the collision?</p>', '["1.5 J","2 J","2.5 J","3 J"]'::jsonb, 'First, we find the final velocity of this inelastic collision. Momentum is conserved, so we can write (1)(6) + (2)(3) = (3)$v$′ since both objects are moving in the same direction. Thus, $v$′ = 4 m/s. The initial kinetic energy of the 1-kg object is 18 J, while the initial kinetic energy of the 2-kg mass is 9 J. Thus, the total initial kinetic energy is 27 J. After the collision, the combined 3-kg object has a velocity of 4 m/s and a final kinetic energy of 24 J. Thus, 3 J of kinetic energy has been lost.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 8, false, 'B', 'Linear Momentum', 'medium', 'A 2-kg mass moving with a velocity of 7 m/s collides elastically with a 4-kg mass moving in the opposite direction at 4 m/s. The 2-kg mass reverses direction after the collision and has a new velocity of 3 m/s. What is the new velocity of the 4-kg mass?</p>', '["−1 m/s","1 m/s","6 m/s","4 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'Momentum is conserved in this elastic collision but the directions are opposite, so we must be careful with negative signs. We therefore write (2)(7) − (4)(4) = −(2)(3) + 4$v$′ and get $v$′ = 1 m/s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 9, false, 'A', 'Linear Momentum', 'hard', 'A mass $m$ is attached to a massless spring with a force constant $k$. The mass rests on a horizontal, frictionless surface. The system is compressed a distance $x$ from the spring’s initial position and then released. The momentum of the mass when the spring passes its equilibrium position is given by</p>', '["$ $-v$ $","$ 0 $","$ v $","$ 2v $"]'::jsonb, 'We set the two energy equations equal to solve for the velocity at the equilibrium position. Thus</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{2}kx^2 = \frac{1}{2}mv^2$</p>
<p class="nump">since no gravitational potential energy is involved, and we write that the velocity is $ p_i = mv $. Now momentum $p$ = <i>mv</i>, so we multiply by $m$ and factor the “mass” back under the radical sign, where it is squared so that we get $ p_f = -mv $.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 10, false, 'B', 'Linear Momentum', 'hard', 'During an inelastic collision between two balls, which of the following statements is correct?</p>', '["Both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved.","Momentum is conserved, but kinetic energy is not conserved.","Momentum is not conserved, but kinetic energy is conserved.","Neither momentum nor kinetic energy is conserved."]'::jsonb, 'Momentum is always conserved in an inelastic collision, and kinetic energy is not conserved because the objects stick together.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 11, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'medium', 'A $0.15\text{ kg}$ baseball moving horizontally at $40\text{ m/s}$ is struck by a bat and rebounds in the opposite direction at $50\text{ m/s}$. If contact lasts $2.0\text{ ms}$, what average force is exerted on the ball?', '["$6,750\\text{ N}$","$750\\text{ N}$","$3,000\\text{ N}$","$13,500\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '$\Delta p = m(v_f - v_i) = 0.15(-50 - 40) = 0.15(-90) = -13.5\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s}$. $F_{avg} = |\Delta p|/\Delta t = 13.5 / 0.002 = 6,750\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 12, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'In an elastic collision between two isolated bodies in one dimension, which quantities are conserved?', '["Linear momentum only","Both total linear momentum and total kinetic energy","Kinetic energy only","Mechanical energy and angular velocity only"]'::jsonb, 'By definition, an elastic collision is one in which no mechanical energy is dissipated into heat or deformation, so both total linear momentum and total kinetic energy are conserved.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 13, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'A $2.0\text{ kg}$ cart moving at $3.0\text{ m/s}$ collides and sticks to a stationary $4.0\text{ kg}$ cart on a frictionless track. What is their common final speed?', '["$1.5\\text{ m/s}$","$2.0\\text{ m/s}$","$1.0\\text{ m/s}$","$0.5\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 'Completely inelastic collision: $m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 = (m_1 + m_2) v_f \implies (2)(3) + 0 = (2+4)v_f \implies 6 = 6v_f \implies v_f = 1.0\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 14, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'medium', 'A rocket in deep space expels exhaust gas at a constant relative speed $u_{ex}$. If its initial mass is $M_0$ and current mass is $M$, what is its increase in speed (Tsiolkovsky equation)?', '["$\\Delta v = u_{ex} \\left(\\dfrac{M_0}{M}\\right)$","$\\Delta v = u_{ex} \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{M}{M_0}\\right)$","$\\Delta v = \\dfrac{1}{2}u_{ex} \\left(1 - \\dfrac{M}{M_0}\\right)$","$\\Delta v = u_{ex} \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{M_0}{M}\\right)$"]'::jsonb, 'The ideal rocket equation derived from momentum conservation: $mdv = -u_{ex}dm \implies \Delta v = u_{ex}\ln(M_0/M)$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 15, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'The area under a force-time graph ($F$ vs $t$) represents:', '["Impulse (change in linear momentum)","Work done (change in kinetic energy)","Power delivered","Total acceleration"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse $J = \int F dt = \Delta p$, which corresponds geometrically to the area under the force-time curve.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 16, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'Two ice skaters initially at rest push off against one another on frictionless ice. Skater A has mass $40\text{ kg}$ and moves east at $3.0\text{ m/s}$. If Skater B has mass $60\text{ kg}$, what is Skater B''s velocity?', '["$3.0\\text{ m/s}$ west","$2.0\\text{ m/s}$ west","$4.5\\text{ m/s}$ west","$1.5\\text{ m/s}$ east"]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of momentum from rest: $m_A v_A + m_B v_B = 0 \implies 40(3.0) + 60 v_B = 0 \implies 120 + 60 v_B = 0 \implies v_B = -2.0\text{ m/s}$ (west).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 17, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'hard', 'In a ballistic pendulum, a bullet of mass $m$ with speed $v$ embeds into a pendulum block of mass $M$, causing it to swing up to height $h$. What is the initial bullet speed in terms of $h$?', '["$v = \\dfrac{m}{m + M}\\sqrt{2gh}$","$v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2gh(m+M)}{m}}$","$v = \\dfrac{m + M}{m}\\sqrt{2gh}$","$v = \\dfrac{m + M}{m}2gh$"]'::jsonb, 'Collision (momentum): $mv = (m+M)V \implies V = \frac{m}{m+M}v$. Swing (energy): $\frac{1}{2}(m+M)V^2 = (m+M)gh \implies V = \sqrt{2gh}$. Equating gives $v = \frac{m+M}{m}\sqrt{2gh}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 18, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'Where is the center of mass of a system consisting of a $1.0\text{ kg}$ mass at $x = 0$ and a $3.0\text{ kg}$ mass at $x = 4.0\text{ m}$?', '["$x = 2.0\\text{ m}$","$x = 1.0\\text{ m}$","$x = 2.5\\text{ m}$","$x = 3.0\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '$x_{cm} = \frac{m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{1(0) + 3(4)}{1 + 3} = \frac{12}{4} = 3.0\text{ m}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 19, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'medium', 'An object explodes into three equal fragments in deep space. Fragment 1 moves along $+x$ with speed $v$, Fragment 2 moves along $+y$ with speed $v$. What is the velocity vector of Fragment 3?', '["$-v\\hat{i} - v\\hat{j}$","$+v\\hat{i} + v\\hat{j}$","$-v\\hat{i} + v\\hat{j}$","$-\\frac{v}{2}\\hat{i} - \\frac{v}{2}\\hat{j}$"]'::jsonb, '$\vec{P}_{tot} = \vec{0} \implies m(v\hat{i}) + m(v\hat{j}) + m\vec{v}_3 = \vec{0} \implies \vec{v}_3 = -v\hat{i} - v\hat{j}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-momentum_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 20, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-momentum', 'easy', 'During a collision, why do safety airbags in automobiles reduce the severity of injury?', '["They reduce the total change in momentum of the occupant","They lengthen the collision time $\\Delta t$, thereby reducing the average impact force for a given impulse","They absorb momentum by making it disappear from the universe","They convert all kinetic energy into gravitational potential energy"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse $J = \Delta p$ is fixed by the initial and final velocity. By extending contact time $\Delta t$, the average impact force $F_{avg} = \Delta p / \Delta t$ is substantially decreased.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 1, false, 'D', 'Rotational Motion', 'easy', '<p>A 45-kg girl is sitting on a seesaw 0.6 m to the left of the central fulcrum. How far to the right of the fulcrum should a 60-kg boy sit so that the seesaw balances horizontally in rotational equilibrium?</p>', '["0.30 m","0.35 m","0.40 m","0.45 m"]'::jsonb, 'To remain in balance, the two torques must be equal. The force on each side is given by the weight, <i>mg</i>. The moment arm distances are 0.6 m and $x$. Since the factor $g$ will appear on both sides of the torque balance equation, we can eliminate it and write</p>
<p class="center1">$(45)(0.6) = (60)x \text{ implies } x = 0.45\text{ m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 2, false, 'A', 'Rotational Motion', 'easy', '<p>A uniform meter stick is pivoted at the 50-cm mark. A 20-g mass is suspended at the 20-cm mark (30 cm from the fulcrum), and a 15-g mass is suspended at the 70-cm mark (20 cm from the fulcrum). What is the position on the meter stick where a 10-g mass should be hung to balance the stick horizontally?</p>', '["80 cm mark","60 cm mark","75 cm mark","25 cm mark","90 cm mark"]'::jsonb, 'Torque equilibrium about the 50-cm pivot: Counter-clockwise torque = (20 g)(30 cm) = 600 g·cm. Clockwise torque from 15 g mass = (15 g)(20 cm) = 300 g·cm. Deficit on clockwise side = 600 - 300 = 300 g·cm. The 10-g mass must be placed at distance d to the right: (10 g)(d) = 300 => d = 30 cm to the right of the 50-cm mark, which is at the 80 cm mark.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 3, false, 'D', 'Rotational Motion', 'easy', '<p>A solid stepped cylinder consists of an outer radius r_1 = 0.4 m and an inner hub radius r_2 = 0.2 m, pivoted on a frictionless axle. A string wound around the outer cylinder pulls with force F_1 = 30 N clockwise, and a string wound around the inner hub pulls with force F_2 = 50 N counter-clockwise. What is the net torque on the cylinder?</p>', '["2.25 N · m","−2.25 N · m","0.5 N · m","−0.5 N · m"]'::jsonb, 'The net torque is given by the vector sum of all torques. \vec{F}<sub>2</sub> provides a counterclockwise positive torque, while \vec{F}<sub>1</sub> provides a clockwise negative torque. Each radius is the necessary moment arm distance. Thus, we have</p>
<p class="center1">$\tau_{\text{net}} = (5)(0.35) - (3)(0.75) = -0.5\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 4, false, 'A', 'Rotational Motion', 'medium', 'What is the net torque about an axis through point <span>$A$</span>?</p>', '["16.8 N · m","15.2 N · m","−5.5 N · m","−7.8 N · m"]'::jsonb, 'In this problem, the net torque about point $A$ implies that the force passing through point $A$ does not contribute to the net torque. Also, we need the components of the remaining forces perpendicular to the beam. From the diagram, we see that the 30-N force acts counterclockwise (positive), while the 10-N force acts clockwise (negative). Thus:</p>
<p class="center1">$\tau_{\text{net}} = (30)(\cos 45^\circ)(1.5) - (10)(\sin 30^\circ)(3) = 16.8\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 5, false, 'B', 'Rotational Motion', 'medium', 'What is the net torque about an axis through point <span>$C$</span>?</p>', '["3.5 N · m","7.5 N · m","−15.2 N · m","5.9 N · m"]'::jsonb, 'In this problem, since the pivot is now set at point $C$, we can eliminate the 30-N force passing through point $C$ as a contributor to the torque. Again, we see that the 20-N force will act in a counterclockwise direction, while the 10-N force will act clockwise. Each force is 1.5 m from the pivot. We also need the component of each force perpendicular to the beam. Thus:</p>
<p class="center1">$\tau_{\text{net}} = (20)(\sin 30^\circ)(1.5) - (10)(\sin 30^\circ)(1.5) = 7.5\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 6, false, 'C', 'Rotational Motion', 'medium', 'Compute the average angular acceleration and the angular displacement during the 2 seconds a rotating object speeds up from 0.5 rad/s to 0.7 rad/s.</p>', '["α = 0.1 rad/$s^{2}$    Δθ = 0.3 radians","α = 0.2 rad/$s^{2}$    Δθ = 0.5 radians","α = 0.1 rad/$s^{2}$    Δθ = 1.2 radians","α = 0.2 rad/$s^{2}$    Δθ = 1.2 radians"]'::jsonb, '$ \tau_{\text{net}} = I\alpha $</p>
<p class="center1">$\Delta\theta = \omega_0 t + \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2 = 0.5(2) + \frac{1}{2}(0.1)(2)^2 = 1 + 0.2 = 1.2\text{ radians}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 7, false, 'D', 'Rotational Motion', 'medium', 'An object with a rotational inertia $I$ experiences a net torque of $T$. If the object is initially at rest, what is its angular speed after 3 seconds of this torque?</p>', '["3<i>I T</i>","9<i>I T</i>","3$I$ /$T$","3$T$/$I$"]'::jsonb, 'Since τ = $I$α and $ L = I\omega $ we can combine and solve for Δω:</p>
<p class="center1">$\Delta\omega = (T/I)\Delta t = 3T/I$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 8, false, 'D', 'Rotational Motion', 'medium', 'If a spinning ball of clay on top of a freely turning frictionless tabletop increases its rotational inertia by 50 percent by bulging outward, what will happen to the rotational speed of the clay and tabletop?</p>', '["It will be half as fast.","It will be 1.5 times faster.","It will be 4 times slower.","It will be slower by a factor of 2/3."]'::jsonb, 'Angular momentum is conserved:</p>
<p class="center1">$I_i \omega_i = I_f \omega_f, I_f = (3/2)I_i, I_i \omega_i = ((3/2)I_i)\omega_f, \omega_f = 2/3\omega_i$</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 9, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'medium', 'A solid cylinder of mass $M$ and radius $R$ rolls without slipping down an incline of height $h$. What is its linear speed at the bottom ($I_{cyl} = \frac{1}{2}MR^2$)?', '["$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{3}gh}$","$\\sqrt{2gh}$","$\\sqrt{gh}$","$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{4}gh}$"]'::jsonb, '$Mgh = \frac{1}{2}Mv^2 + \frac{1}{2}I\omega^2 = \frac{1}{2}Mv^2 + \frac{1}{2}(\frac{1}{2}MR^2)(v/R)^2 = \frac{3}{4}Mv^2 \implies v = \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}gh}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 10, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'A uniform rod of length $L$ and mass $M$ is pivoted at one end. What is its moment of inertia about the pivot?', '["$\\frac{1}{12}ML^2$","$\\frac{1}{3}ML^2$","$\\frac{1}{2}ML^2$","$ML^2$"]'::jsonb, 'By the parallel axis theorem: $I = I_{cm} + M(L/2)^2 = \frac{1}{12}ML^2 + \frac{1}{4}ML^2 = \frac{1}{3}ML^2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 11, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'hard', 'A figure skater spins with arms outstretched at angular velocity $\omega_0$. When she pulls her arms inward, her rotational inertia decreases to half its original value. What is her new rotational kinetic energy?', '["$K_0$","$4K_0$","$2K_0$","$0.5K_0$"]'::jsonb, 'Angular momentum is conserved: $I_0\omega_0 = (I_0/2)\omega \implies \omega = 2\omega_0$. Kinetic energy $K = \frac{1}{2}I\omega^2 = \frac{1}{2}(I_0/2)(2\omega_0)^2 = 2(\frac{1}{2}I_0\omega_0^2) = 2K_0$ (work is done by her muscles).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 12, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'A torque $\tau = 12\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}$ acts on a flywheel with moment of inertia $I = 3.0\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m}^2$. What is the angular acceleration of the flywheel?', '["$36\\text{ rad/s}^2$","$0.25\\text{ rad/s}^2$","$6.0\\text{ rad/s}^2$","$4.0\\text{ rad/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, 'Newton''s second law for rotation: $\tau = I\alpha \implies \alpha = \tau / I = 12 / 3.0 = 4.0\text{ rad/s}^2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 13, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'medium', 'A solid sphere, a solid cylinder, and a thin hoop of identical mass $M$ and radius $R$ are released simultaneously from rest at the top of an incline. Which object reaches the bottom first?', '["The solid sphere","The solid cylinder","The thin hoop","They all arrive at the same time"]'::jsonb, 'Acceleration $a = \frac{g\sin\theta}{1 + I/(MR^2)}$. The object with the smallest fraction $I/(MR^2)$ has the greatest acceleration. Sphere ($2/5=0.4$) < cylinder ($1/2=0.5$) < hoop ($1.0$), so the solid sphere wins.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 14, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'A horizontal disk of radius $R = 0.50\text{ m}$ rotates at constant angular speed $\omega = 4.0\text{ rad/s}$. What is the centripetal acceleration of a point on its outer rim?', '["$2.0\\text{ m/s}^2$","$8.0\\text{ m/s}^2$","$16\\text{ m/s}^2$","$4.0\\text{ m/s}^2$"]'::jsonb, '$a_c = \omega^2 R = (4.0)^2 \times 0.50 = 16 \times 0.50 = 8.0\text{ m/s}^2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 15, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'medium', 'A particle of mass $m$ moves with constant velocity $\vec{v} = v\hat{i}$ along the line $y = b$. What is its angular momentum with respect to the origin?', '["$+m v b\\hat{i}$","Zero","$-m v b\\hat{k}$","$+m v b^2\\hat{k}$"]'::jsonb, '$\vec{L} = \vec{r} \times \vec{p} = (x\hat{i} + b\hat{j}) \times (mv\hat{i}) = -mv b(\hat{j} \times \hat{i}) = -mv b\hat{k}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 16, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'What is the condition for static equilibrium of a rigid body?', '["Net external force must be zero only","Net external torque must be zero only","Mechanical energy must be minimized to zero","Both net external force and net external torque must equal zero: $\\sum\\vec{F} = \\vec{0}$ and $\\sum\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{0}$"]'::jsonb, 'Complete static equilibrium requires translational equilibrium ($\sum\vec{F} = \vec{0}$) and rotational equilibrium ($\sum\vec{\tau} = \vec{0}$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 17, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'medium', 'A uniform meter stick of mass $0.20\text{ kg}$ is balanced at its center ($50\text{ cm}$). If a $0.30\text{ kg}$ mass is placed at $20\text{ cm}$, where must a $0.45\text{ kg}$ mass be placed to restore balance?', '["$70\\text{ cm}$","$65\\text{ cm}$","$80\\text{ cm}$","$75\\text{ cm}$"]'::jsonb, 'Torque about center ($50\text{ cm}$): $(0.30)(50 - 20) = (0.45)(x - 50) \implies 0.30(30) = 0.45(x - 50) \implies 9.0 = 0.45(x - 50) \implies x - 50 = 20 \implies x = 70\text{ cm}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 18, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'medium', 'A wheel accelerates from rest with constant angular acceleration $\alpha = 2.0\text{ rad/s}^2$. Through how many revolutions does it turn in $10\text{ s}$?', '["Approximately $31.8\\text{ rev}$","Approximately $15.9\\text{ rev}$","$100\\text{ rev}$","$50\\text{ rev}$"]'::jsonb, '$\theta = \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2 = \frac{1}{2}(2.0)(10^2) = 100\text{ radians}$. In revolutions: $N = 100 / (2\pi) \approx 15.92\text{ rev}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 19, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'A rolling wheel has forward velocity $v_{cm}$. What is the instantaneous velocity of the contact point where the wheel touches the ground?', '["$v_{cm}$ forward","$2v_{cm}$ forward","Zero","$v_{cm}$ backward"]'::jsonb, 'In pure rolling without slipping, the bottom contact point is instantaneously at rest relative to the surface ($v = v_{cm} - \omega R = 0$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-rotation_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 20, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-rotation', 'easy', 'How does the work-energy theorem apply to rotating rigid bodies?', '["Torque does no work on a rotating body","Work is always conserved as linear kinetic energy","Rotational work equals $\\tau / \\theta$","The net work done by external torques equals the change in rotational kinetic energy: $W_{net} = \\Delta\\left(\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2\\right)$"]'::jsonb, 'Rotational work $W = \int \tau d\theta = \Delta K_{rot} = \frac{1}{2}I\omega_f^2 - \frac{1}{2}I\omega_i^2$.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 1, false, 'B', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'easy', 'What is the length of a pendulum whose period, at the equator, is 1 s?</p>', '["0.15 m","0.25 m","0.30 m","0.45 m"]'::jsonb, 'If we use the formula for the period of a pendulum, $ T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} $, and square both sides, we can solve for the length:</p>
<p class="center1">$\ell = T^2 \frac{g}{4\pi^2}$</p>
<p class="exp">Using the values given in the question, we find that <i>ℓ</i> = 0.25 m.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 2, false, 'D', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'easy', 'On a planet, an astronaut determines the acceleration of gravity by means of a pendulum. She observes that the 1-m-long pendulum has a period of 1.5 s. The acceleration of gravity, in meters per second squared, on the planet is</p>', '["7.5","15.2","10.2","17.5"]'::jsonb, 'Again using the formula for the period of a pendulum, this time we solve for the magnitude of the acceleration of gravity:</p>
<p class="center1">$g = 4\frac{\pi\ell}{T^2}$</p>
<p class="exp">Using the values given in the question, we find that $g$ = 17.5 m/$s^{2}$.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 3, false, 'C', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'easy', 'When a 0.05-kg mass is attached to a vertical spring, it is observed that the spring stretches 0.03 m. The system is then placed horizontally on a frictionless surface and set into simple harmonic motion. What is the period of the oscillations?</p>', '["0.75 s","0.12 s","0.35 s","1.3 s"]'::jsonb, 'We first find the spring constant. From $F$ = <i>kx</i>, we see that</p>
<p class="center1">$F = mg = (0.05)(9.8) = 0.49\text{ N}$</p>
<p class="exp">Thus,</p>
<p class="center1">$k = \frac{F}{x} = \frac{0.49}{0.03} = 16.3\text{ N/m}$</p>
<p class="exp">Now the period is given by the formula $T =  T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} $. Using the values given in the question, we find that $T$ = 0.35 s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 4, false, 'A', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'medium', 'A mass of 0.5 kg is connected to a massless spring with a force constant $k$ of 50 N/m. The system is oscillating on a frictionless, horizontal surface. If the amplitude of the oscillations is 2 cm, the total energy of the system is</p>', '["0.01 J","0.1 J","0.5 J","0.3 J"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for total energy is $E$ = (1/2)<i>kA</i><sup>2</sup>. Using the values given in the question, we get $E$ = 0.01 J.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 5, false, 'A', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'medium', 'A mass of 0.3 kg is connected to a massless spring with a force constant $k$ of 20 N/m. The system oscillates horizontally on a frictionless surface with an amplitude of 4 cm. What is the velocity of the mass when it is 2 cm from its equilibrium position?</p>', '["0.28 m/s","0.08 m/s","0.52 m/s","0.15 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'The formula for any intermediary velocity is $v =  f = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} $. Before substituting the known values, we must change centimeters to meters: 4 cm = 0.04 m and 2 cm = 0.02 m. Then, using these values for amplitude and position, we find that $v$ = 0.28 m/s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 6, false, 'D', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'medium', 'If the length of a simple pendulum is doubled, its period will</p>', '["decrease by 2","increase by 2","$decrease by \\sqrt{2}$","$increase by \\sqrt{2}$"]'::jsonb, '$Since the period of a pendulum varies directly as the square root of the length, if the length is doubled, the period increases by \sqrt{2}.</p></li>
<li>$'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 7, false, 'A', 'Oscillatory Motion', 'medium', 'A 2-kg mass is oscillating horizontally on a frictionless surface when attached to a spring. The total energy of the system is observed to be 10 J. If the mass is replaced by a 4-kg mass, but the amplitude of oscillations and the spring remain the same, the total energy of the system will be</p>', '["10 J","5 J","20 J","3.3 J"]'::jsonb, 'The maximum energy in the mass-spring system can be expressed independently of the mass; it depends on the force constant and the square of the amplitude. Thus, if the spring and the amplitude of oscillations remain the same, so will the total energy of the system (10 J).</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 8, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'A block on a horizontal spring oscillates with period $T_0$. If the mass of the block is quadrupled, what is the new oscillation period?', '["$2T_0$","$4T_0$","$0.5T_0$","$\\sqrt{2}T_0$"]'::jsonb, '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$. When mass increases by a factor of 4, the period increases by $\sqrt{4} = 2$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 9, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'In simple harmonic motion described by $x(t) = A\cos(\omega t)$, at what position is the magnitude of the restoring acceleration maximized?', '["At the equilibrium position ($x = 0$)","At the extreme amplitudes ($x = \\pm A$)","At $x = \\pm A/2$","Acceleration is constant throughout the motion"]'::jsonb, '$a(t) = -\omega^2 x$. Acceleration is directly proportional to displacement; its magnitude reaches maximum $|a_{max}| = \omega^2 A$ at $x = \pm A$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 10, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'medium', 'A simple pendulum of length $L$ oscillates with period $2.0\text{ s}$ on Earth. What length would be required for the pendulum to have the same period on the Moon ($g_{moon} = g/6$)?', '["$6L$","$L/\\sqrt{6}$","$L/6$","$L/36$"]'::jsonb, '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$. For constant period $T$, $L/g = \text{const} \implies L_{moon} = L(g_{moon}/g) = L/6$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 11, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'medium', 'At what displacement in simple harmonic motion is the kinetic energy equal to the potential energy?', '["$x = \\pm \\dfrac{A}{2}$","$x = 0$","$x = \\pm \\dfrac{A}{4}$","$x = \\pm \\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$"]'::jsonb, '$U = \frac{1}{2}kx^2 = \frac{1}{2}E_{tot} = \frac{1}{2}(\frac{1}{2}kA^2) \implies x^2 = A^2/2 \implies x = \pm A/\sqrt{2}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 12, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'A mass-spring system oscillates with amplitude $A$. If the amplitude is doubled to $2A$, how does the total mechanical energy change?', '["It increases by a factor of 4","It doubles","It remains unchanged","It increases by a factor of 8"]'::jsonb, 'Total energy in SHM is $E = \frac{1}{2}kA^2$. Energy is proportional to $A^2$, so doubling amplitude quadruples total energy ($2^2 = 4$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 13, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'medium', 'What is the phase difference between displacement and velocity in simple harmonic motion?', '["$\\pi\\text{ radians}$ ($180^\\circ$)","$\\pi/2\\text{ radians}$ ($90^\\circ$)","$0\\text{ radians}$","$2\\pi\\text{ radians}$"]'::jsonb, '$x(t) = A\cos(\omega t) \implies v(t) = -A\omega\sin(\omega t) = A\omega\cos(\omega t + \pi/2)$. Velocity leads displacement by $\pi/2$ radians ($90^\circ$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 14, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'hard', 'A physical pendulum consists of a uniform disk of radius $R$ pivoted at its edge. What is its period of small oscillations ($I_{pivot} = \frac{3}{2}MR^2$)?', '["$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{g}}$","$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2R}{3g}}$","$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3R}{2g}}$","$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{2g}}$"]'::jsonb, '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{I / (mg d)}$. Here $d = R$ and $I = \frac{3}{2}MR^2$. Thus $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{3MR^2/2}{MgR}} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{3R}{2g}}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 15, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'In damped harmonic motion, what causes the oscillation amplitude to decay exponentially over time?', '["Continuous decrease in the spring constant $k$","Continuous increase in the system mass","Gravitational wave emission","Dissipation of mechanical energy into thermal energy by non-conservative resistive forces"]'::jsonb, 'Damping forces (viscosity, friction) perform negative work on the oscillator, transferring mechanical energy into thermal energy.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 16, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'medium', 'A vertical spring stretches by $0.20\text{ m}$ when a $2.0\text{ kg}$ object is suspended at rest from it. What is the frequency of vertical oscillations ($g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$1.1\\text{ Hz}$","$2.2\\text{ Hz}$","$0.55\\text{ Hz}$","$4.9\\text{ Hz}$"]'::jsonb, '$k = mg / \Delta y = 2.0(9.8)/0.20 = 98\text{ N/m}$. Frequency $f = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{k/m} = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{98/2.0} = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{49} = \frac{7}{2\pi} \approx 1.11\text{ Hz}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 17, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'Resonance in a driven harmonic oscillator occurs when:', '["The driving force is completely out of phase with velocity","The driving frequency matches the natural frequency of the system, maximizing oscillation amplitude","The damping coefficient approaches infinity","The restoring force vanishes completely"]'::jsonb, 'Resonance occurs when the driving frequency equals the system''s undamped natural frequency $\omega_0$, allowing optimal energy transfer and maximal amplitude.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 18, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'A particle in SHM passes through the equilibrium position with speed $v_{max} = 3.0\text{ m/s}$. If its angular frequency is $\omega = 6.0\text{ rad/s}$, what is the oscillation amplitude?', '["$2.0\\text{ m}$","$18\\text{ m}$","$0.50\\text{ m}$","$1.0\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '$v_{max} = \omega A \implies A = v_{max} / \omega = 3.0 / 6.0 = 0.50\text{ m}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 19, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'medium', 'Which differential equation characterizes simple harmonic motion of position $x(t)$?', '["$\\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2} - \\omega^2 x = 0$","$\\dfrac{dx}{dt} + \\omega x = 0$","$\\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2} + \\omega \\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 = 0$","$\\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2} + \\omega^2 x = 0$"]'::jsonb, 'Newton''s second law for a linear restoring force: $m(d^2x/dt^2) = -kx \implies d^2x/dt^2 + (k/m)x = 0$, where $\omega^2 = k/m$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-shm_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 20, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-shm', 'easy', 'A simple pendulum has period $T$. What happens to the period if the mass of the bob is doubled without altering the string length?', '["The period remains unchanged","The period doubles","The period increases by $\\sqrt{2}$","The period decreases by half"]'::jsonb, 'The period of a simple pendulum $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$ is independent of the mass of the pendulum bob.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q1', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 1, false, 'A', 'Fluids', 'easy', 'The rate of flow of a liquid from a hole in a container depends on all of the following except</p>', '["the density of the liquid","the height of the liquid above the hole","the area of the hole","the acceleration of gravity"]'::jsonb, 'The flow rate of a liquid from a hole does not depend on the density of the liquid.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q2', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 2, false, 'B', 'Fluids', 'easy', 'A person is standing on a railroad station platform when a high-speed train passes by. The person will tend to be</p>', '["pushed away from the train","pulled in toward the train","pushed upward into the air","pulled down into the ground"]'::jsonb, 'Because of the Bernoulli effect, the speeding train reduces the air pressure between the person and the train. This pressure difference creates a force tending to pull the person into the train. Be very careful when standing on a train platform!</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q3', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 3, false, 'D', 'Fluids', 'easy', 'Bernoulli’s equation is based on which law of physics?</p>', '["Conservation of linear momentum","Conservation of angular momentum","Newton’s first law of motion","Conservation of energy"]'::jsonb, 'Bernoulli’s principle was developed as an application of conservation of energy.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q4', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 4, false, 'A', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'Which of the following expressions represents the power generated by a liquid flowing out of a hole of area $A$ with a velocity <span>$v$</span>?</p>', '["(pressure) × (rate of flow)","(pressure)/(rate of flow)","(rate of flow)/(pressure)","(pressure) × (velocity)/(area)"]'::jsonb, 'Power is expressed in J/s. The product of pressure (force divided by area) and flow rate ($m^{3}$/s) leads to newtons times meters over seconds (J/s).</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q5', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 5, false, 'D', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements is an expression of the equation of continuity?</p>', '["Rate of flow equals the product of velocity and cross-sectional area.","Rate of flow depends on the height of the fluid above the hole.","Pressure in a static fluid is transmitted uniformly throughout.","Fluid flows faster through a narrower pipe."]'::jsonb, 'The equation of continuity states that the product of velocity and area is a constant for a given fluid. A consequence of this is that a fluid moves faster through a narrower pipe.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q6', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 6, false, 'D', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'A moving fluid has an average pressure of 600 Pa as it exits a circular hole with a radius of 2 cm at a velocity of 60 m/s. What is the approximate power generated by the fluid?</p>', '["32 W","62 W","1,200 W","45 W"]'::jsonb, 'The power is equal to the product of the pressure and the flow rate. The flow rate is equal to the product of the velocity and the cross-sectional area (which is a circle of radius 0.02 m). The area is given by π$r^{2}$ = 1.256 × 10<sup>−3</sup> $m^{2}$. When we multiply this area by the velocity and the pressure, we get 45.2 W as a measure of the generated power.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q7', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 7, false, 'D', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'Alcohol has a specific gravity of 0.79. If a barometer consisting of an open-ended tube placed in a dish of alcohol is used at sea level, to what height in the tube will the alcohol rise?</p>', '["8.1 m","7.9 m","15.2 m","13.1 m"]'::jsonb, 'The pressure exerted by the air is balanced by the column of liquid alcohol in equilibrium:</p>
<p class="center1">$h = \frac{P}{\rho g} = \frac{1.01 \times 10^5\text{ N/m}^2}{(790\text{ kg/m}^3)(9.8\text{ m/s}^2)} = 13.1\text{ m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q8', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 8, false, 'A', 'Fluids', 'medium', 'An ice cube is dropped into a mixed drink containing alcohol and water. The ice cube sinks to the bottom. From this, you can conclude</p>', '["that the drink is mostly alcohol","that the drink is mostly water","that the drink is equally mixed with water and alcohol","nothing, unless you know how much liquid is present"]'::jsonb, 'Since the density of alcohol is less than that of water, ice floats “lower” in alcohol than in water. From the given information, the drink appears to be mostly alcohol.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q9', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 9, false, 'B', 'Fluids', 'hard', 'A 2-N force is used to push a small piston 10 cm downward in a simple hydraulic machine. If the opposite large piston rises by 0.5 cm, what is the maximum weight the large piston can lift?</p>', '["2 N","40 N","20 N","4 N"]'::jsonb, 'We need to use the conservation of work-energy in this problem. The work done on the small piston must equal the work done by the large piston. Since the ratio of displacements is 20:1, the large piston will be able to support a maximum load of 40 N (since 2 N × 20 = 40 N).</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q10', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 10, false, 'B', 'Fluids', 'hard', 'Balsa wood with an average density of 130 kg/$m^{3}$ is floating in pure water. What percentage of the wood is submerged?</p>', '["87%","13%","50%","25%"]'::jsonb, 'The percentage submerged is given by the ratio of its density to that of pure water (1,000 kg/$m^{3}$). Thus, 130/1,000 = 0.13 = 13%.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>
</section>
<section>'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q11', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 11, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'Water flows through a horizontal pipe of varying diameter. At point 1, diameter is $4.0\text{ cm}$ and speed is $2.0\text{ m/s}$. What is the flow speed at point 2 where diameter narrows to $2.0\text{ cm}$?', '["$8.0\\text{ m/s}$","$4.0\\text{ m/s}$","$1.0\\text{ m/s}$","$16\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 'Continuity equation for incompressible flow: $A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2 \implies d_1^2 v_1 = d_2^2 v_2 \implies (4)^2(2) = (2)^2 v_2 \implies 32 = 4v_2 \implies v_2 = 8.0\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q12', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 12, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'easy', 'A solid cube of wood with side length $0.10\text{ m}$ floats in water ($\rho_w = 1000\text{ kg/m}^3$) with $70\%$ of its volume submerged. What is the density of the wood?', '["$300\\text{ kg/m}^3$","$700\\text{ kg/m}^3$","$850\\text{ kg/m}^3$","$500\\text{ kg/m}^3$"]'::jsonb, 'By Archimedes'' principle for floating equilibrium: $mg = B \implies \rho_{wood} V g = \rho_w (0.70 V) g \implies \rho_{wood} = 0.70 \rho_w = 700\text{ kg/m}^3$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q13', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 13, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'easy', 'According to Bernoulli''s principle, for horizontal steady flow of an ideal fluid, what happens to the static pressure in a region where fluid velocity increases?', '["Static pressure increases","Static pressure remains constant","Static pressure decreases","Static pressure drops to absolute zero"]'::jsonb, 'Bernoulli''s equation: $P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho g y = \text{const}$. On a horizontal streamline, if kinetic energy density increases ($v \uparrow$), static pressure must decrease ($P \downarrow$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q14', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 14, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'What is the absolute hydrostatic pressure at a depth of $20\text{ m}$ in fresh water ($\rho = 1000\text{ kg/m}^3$, $g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2$, $P_{atm} = 1.01 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$)?', '["$1.96 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$3.98 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$1.01 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$","$2.97 \\times 10^5\\text{ Pa}$"]'::jsonb, '$P = P_{atm} + \rho g h = 1.01 \times 10^5 + 1000(9.8)(20) = 1.01 \times 10^5 + 1.96 \times 10^5 = 2.97 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q15', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 15, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'A hydraulic lift has an input piston of area $A_1 = 0.02\text{ m}^2$ and an output piston of area $A_2 = 0.80\text{ m}^2$. What force applied to the input piston can lift a $1,600\text{ kg}$ vehicle ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$400\\text{ N}$","$200\\text{ N}$","$800\\text{ N}$","$1,600\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, 'Pascal''s principle: $F_1 / A_1 = F_2 / A_2 \implies F_1 = F_2(A_1 / A_2) = (1600 \times 10)(0.02 / 0.80) = 16000 \times 0.025 = 400\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q16', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 16, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'A large open water tank develops a small leak hole at depth $h = 5.0\text{ m}$ below the water surface. What is the efflux speed of water exiting the hole (Torricelli''s law, $g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$7.0\\text{ m/s}$","$9.9\\text{ m/s}$","$14\\text{ m/s}$","$49\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '$v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2 \times 9.8 \times 5.0} = \sqrt{98} \approx 9.899\text{ m/s} \approx 9.9\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q17', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 17, false, 'C', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'A metal anchor of mass $26\text{ kg}$ and volume $0.010\text{ m}^3$ is completely submerged in sea water ($\rho_{sea} = 1025\text{ kg/m}^3$). What is the buoyant force acting on it ($g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$254.8\\text{ N}$","$154.3\\text{ N}$","$100.5\\text{ N}$","$50.2\\text{ N}$"]'::jsonb, '$B = \rho_{fluid} V_{sub} g = 1025 \times 0.010 \times 9.8 = 10.25 \times 9.8 = 100.45\text{ N} \approx 100.5\text{ N}$.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q18', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 18, false, 'D', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'easy', 'Why does a curveball curve when pitched with topspin or sidespin in air (Magnus effect)?', '["Buoyant force increases on the top side","Gravitational pull bends sideways","Air drag ceases to act entirely","Differential airflow velocity causes a pressure difference between opposite sides of the spinning ball"]'::jsonb, 'Spin deflects the boundary air layer, increasing air speed on one side (reducing pressure) and decreasing air speed on the other side (increasing pressure), creating a lateral aerodynamic force.'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q19', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 19, false, 'A', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'easy', 'What is gauge pressure?', '["The difference between absolute pressure and atmospheric pressure: $P_{gauge} = P_{abs} - P_{atm}$","The total pressure exerted by the fluid including atmospheric pressure","The pressure measured in a perfect vacuum","The vapor pressure of water at boiling point"]'::jsonb, 'Gauge pressure measures pressure relative to local ambient atmospheric pressure ($P_{gauge} = P - P_{atm}$).'),
  ('ap-topic-phys1-fluids_q20', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 20, false, 'B', 'ap-topic-phys1-fluids', 'medium', 'A U-tube manometer contains oil and water in static balance. If water of density $1.0\text{ g/cm}^3$ has height $h_w = 8.0\text{ cm}$, what height of oil of density $0.80\text{ g/cm}^3$ balances it?', '["$6.4\\text{ cm}$","$10.0\\text{ cm}$","$8.0\\text{ cm}$","$12.5\\text{ cm}$"]'::jsonb, 'Equal pressure at interface: $\rho_w g h_w = \rho_{oil} g h_{oil} \implies h_{oil} = h_w (\rho_w / \rho_{oil}) = 8.0(1.0 / 0.80) = 10.0\text{ cm}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q1', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 1, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Two objects are thrown vertically upward from the same initial height. One object has twice the initial velocity of the other. Neglecting any air resistance, the object with the greater initial velocity will rise to a maximum height that is</p>', '["twice that of the other object, assuming they have the same mass","twice that of the other object, independent of their masses","four times that of the other object, assuming they have the same mass","four times that of the other object, independent of their masses"]'::jsonb, 'Twice the initial vertical velocity will give twice the time in flight. Average vertical velocity will also be doubled. Displacement is the product of these two: 2 × 2 = 4.</p>
<p class="numpt">Alternatively, one could use $ v^2 = v_0^2 + 2ad $, since $v$ at the maximum height is zero. Since $v_{0}$ is doubled and then squared, the vertical displacement $d$ must be 4 times bigger.</p>
<p class="numpt">Free-fall problems are independent of mass.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q2', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 2, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A 2-kilogram cart has a velocity of 4 meters per second to the right. It collides with a 5-kilogram cart moving to the left at 1 meter per second. After the collision, the two carts stick together. Can the magnitude and the direction of the velocity of the two carts after the collision be determined from the given information?</p>', '["No, since the collision is inelastic, we must know the energy lost.","Yes, the collision is elastic: 3/7 m/s left.","Yes, the collision is inelastic: 3/7 m/s right.","Yes, the speed is not 3/7 m/s."]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of momentum:</p> m_1 v_{1i} + m_2 v_{2i} = (m_1 + m_2) v_f \implies (2)(4) + (5)(-1) = 7 v_f \implies v_f = \frac{3}{7}\text{ m/s (to the right)} $$
<p class="numpt">The collision is inelastic since the carts stick together.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q3', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 3, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Projectile $X$ is launched at a 30-degree angle above the horizon with a speed of 100 m/s. Projectile $Y$ is launched at a 60-degree angle with the same speed. Which of the following correctly compares the horizontal range and maximum altitude obtained by these two projectiles?</p>
<p><img alt="" class="w71" role="presentation" src="images/f179-01.jpg"/></p>', '["Choice (A)","Choice (B)","Choice (C)","Choice (D)"]'::jsonb, 'Higher altitude is strictly a function of $V$<sub>$Y$</sub> (Projectile $Y$). Range is a function of both $V$<sub>$X$</sub> and $V$<sub>$Y$</sub> such that the angles equally above and below 45 degrees (the max angle for range) will result in equal horizontal displacement. Note that 30 degrees and 60 degrees are both 15 degrees off from 45 degrees.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q4', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 4, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A 5-kg mass is sitting at rest on a horizontal surface. A horizontal force of 10 N will start the mass moving. What is the best statement about the coefficient and type of friction between the mass and the surface?</p>', '["&gt;0.20 static","&lt;0.20 static","&lt;0.20 kinetic","&gt;0.20 kinetic"]'::jsonb, 'Since the 10 N force will start the mass moving, this must be greater than the static friction force holding the mass in place. The maximum <i>static</i> friction must be 10 N or less.</p> \mu_s \ge \frac{F}{mg} = \frac{10}{(5)(9.8)} = 0.204 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q5', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 5, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Which of the following graphs represents an object <i>moving</i> with no net force acting on it?</p>', '["<img alt=\"\" class=\"w29 inline\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f180-01.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w29 inline\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f180-02.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w34 inline\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f180-03.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w29 inline\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f180-04.jpg\"/>"]'::jsonb, 'The object is moving, so the velocity is not zero. The object is not accelerating, so velocity is constant.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q6', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 6, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A projectile is launched horizontally with an initial velocity $v_{0}$ from a height $h$. If it is assumed that there is no air resistance, which of the following expressions represents the vertical position of the projectile? In other words, $y$($x$) = ?</p>', '["(h - gv_0^2/2x^2)","(h - gv/2v_0^2)","(h - gx^2/2v_0^2)","(h - gx^2/v_0^2)"]'::jsonb, 'In general:</p> a = \frac{F}{m_1 + m_2} = \frac{10}{4 + 1} = 2\text{ m/s}^2 $$
<p class="numpt">Solve for $t$ in the last equation. Then plug back into the first equation and substitute in $y_{0} = h$, $v$<sub>$y$</sub><sub>0</sub> = 0, and $v$<sub>$x$</sub><sub>0</sub> = $v_{0}$:</p> F_{4\text{ on }1} = m_1 a = (1\text{ kg})(2\text{ m/s}^2) = 2\text{ N} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q7', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 7, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Which of the following measurements would allow you to determine the maximum velocity experienced by the mass?</p>', '["No additional information is required.","Minimum velocity","Maximum acceleration","None of these would allow you to determine maximum velocity."]'::jsonb, 'Maximum acceleration allows you to determine maximum displacement:</p>
<p class="center1">$ma = kA$</p>
<p class="numpt">Knowing the amplitude allows you to determine easily the maximum speed via energy conservation:</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{2}kA^2 = \frac{1}{2}mv^2$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q8', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 8, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Which of the following statements concerning the oscillatory motion described above is correct? (All statements refer to magnitudes.)</p>', '["The maximum velocity and maximum acceleration occur at the same time.","The maximum velocity occurs when the acceleration is a minimum.","The velocity is always directly proportional to the displacement.","The maximum velocity occurs when the displacement is a maximum."]'::jsonb, 'Maximum velocities happen when going through the equilibrium point (zero acceleration and zero displacement): all kinetic energy (<i>KE</i>) and no potential energy (<i>PE</i>).</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q9', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 9, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A bullet of known mass ($m_{1}$) is fired vertically into an initially stationary wood block of known mass ($m_{2}$). The resulting wood + bullet combined system is then measured to rise to a maximum height of $h$. Can the initial speed of the bullet be calculated from this information?</p>', '["Yes. Solve ((m_1 + m_2)gh = \frac{1}{2}(m_1)v^2)","Yes. Solve the momentum conservation of collision first and the energy conservation of the rising combination second.","No. We don’t know if momentum is conserved during this collision.","No. We don’t know enough details about the mechanical energy lost during the collision."]'::jsonb, 'Momentum is conserved during the collision, which enables us to solve for an initial upward velocity of the combination. Then energy conservation can be used to relate the height to that initial upward velocity. Choice (A) cannot be used because some unknown amount of mechanical energy will be lost by the bullet embedding itself in the wood.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q10', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 10, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A 10-kg mass is being pushed horizontally by a constant force along a rough surface ($\mu$<sub>$k$</sub> = 0.1) at constant velocity. Which of the following is the best statement regarding the constant force ($f$)?</p>', '["$f$ = 10 N","$f$ &gt; 10 N","$f$ &lt; 10 N","$f$ &gt; 1 N"]'::jsonb, 'Constant velocity means no acceleration, so $ F = ma $.</p>
<p class="center1">$F_{\text{push}} - F_{\text{friction}} = 0, F_{\text{push}} - \mu_k N = F_{\text{push}} - \mu_k mg = 0, F_{\text{push}} = \mu_k mg = (0.1)(10\text{ kg})(10\text{ m/s}^2) = 10\text{ N}$</p></li>
<li>')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q11', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 11, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A 10-newton force is applied to two masses, 4 kilograms and 1 kilogram, respectively, that are in contact as shown below. The horizontal motion is along a frictionless plane. What is the magnitude of the contact force between the two masses?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w68" role="presentation" src="images/f181-01.jpg"/></p>', '["10 N","8 N","6 N","2 N"]'::jsonb, 'First find the acceleration of the system:</p> W = \Delta KE \implies F d = \frac{1}{2}m v_f^2 $$
<p class="numpt">The contact force, $P$, is the only force felt by the 1-kg mass:</p> \frac{1}{2}k x^2 = mgh $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q12', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 12, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An object with mass $m$ is dropped from height $h$ above the ground. While neglecting air resistance, which formula best describes the power generated if the object takes time $t$ to fall?</p>', '["<i>mgh</i>","<i>mght</i>","<i>mg</i><sup>2</sup>$t$/2","<i>mgh</i>/$t$"]'::jsonb, '$P$ = work/$t$ = <i>Fd</i>/$t$ = <i>mgh</i>/$t$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q13', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 13, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A 1,500-kilogram car has a velocity of 25 meters per second. If it is brought to a stop by a nonconstant force in 10 to 15 seconds, can the magnitude of the impulse applied be determined?</p>', '["Yes, it is 37,500 N · s.","No, you need to know the details about the nonconstant force.","No, you must know the exact duration of the impulse.","No, you must know the average force during and the duration of the impulse."]'::jsonb, 'Impulse can be found by <i>Ft</i> if the details of the force are known or, alternatively, by:</p> h = \frac{k x^2}{2mg} $$
<p class="numpt">Ignore the minus sign as the question asks about magnitude.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q14', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 14, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A block of mass $M$ rests on a rough incline, as shown below. The angle of elevation of the incline is increased until an angle of $\theta$ is reached. At that angle, the mass begins to slide down the incline. Which of the following is an expression for the coefficient of static friction $\mu$?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w47" role="presentation" src="images/f181-02.jpg"/></p>', '["tan $\\theta$","sin $\\theta$","cos $\\theta$","1/(cos $\\theta$)"]'::jsonb, 'When the block is at the maximum height, static friction is obtained:</p> J = \int F dt = \text{Area under } F\text{-}t \text{ graph} = \Delta p $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q15', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 15, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A pendulum of a given length swings back and forth a certain number of times per second. If the pendulum now swings back and forth the same number of times but in twice the time, the length of the pendulum should be</p>', '["doubled","quartered","quadrupled","halved"]'::jsonb, 'Doubling the period requires a quadrupling of length:</p> v_f = \frac{\Delta p}{m} = \frac{\text{Area}}{9} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q16', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 16, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'This graph of force versus time shows how the force acts on an object of mass $m$ for a total time of $T$ seconds. If the mass begins at rest, which is the correct method to find the final speed of the mass?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w60" role="presentation" src="images/f182-01.jpg"/></p>', '["Average value of this graph times total time divided by mass","Area under this graph divided by mass","Since the final force is zero, the object is at rest after time $T$","Average slope of this graph divided by mass"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse = area of $F$ vs. $T$ graph = Δ$p$ = <i>mv</i><sub>$f$</sub> − 0</p>
<p class="numpt">Solving for $v$<sub>$f$</sub> = (area under graph)/$m$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q17', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 17, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A child of unknown mass is on a swing of unknown length that varies in height from 75 cm at its lowest height above the ground to a maximum height of 225 cm above the ground. Is there enough information to find the speed of the swing at its lowest point?</p>', '["No, the child’s mass must be known.","No, the length of the swing must be known to determine the centripetal acceleration.","Yes, it is 5.5 m/s.","Yes, it is 4 m/s."]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of energy:</p> T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q18', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 18, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'The gravitational force of attraction between two identical masses is 36 N when the masses are separated by a distance of 3 m. If the distance between them is reduced to 1 m, which of the following is true about the net gravitational field strength due to both masses being at the halfway point?</p>', '["It is 9 times stronger total.","Not enough information is given to determine net gravitational field strength.","Each mass’s gravitation is 9 times stronger, so the net gravitational field strength is 18 times stronger.","It is zero."]'::jsonb, 'Gravitational field strength halfway between any two equal masses is always zero as each contributes oppositely directed gravitational fields.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q19', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 19, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A 200-kilogram cart rests on top of a frictionless hill as shown below. Can the impulse required to stop the cart when it is at the top of the 10-meter hill be calculated?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w72" role="presentation" src="images/f182-02.jpg"/></p>', '["No, more information about friction is required.","No, more information about the impulse is required.","Yes, calculate the velocity from free-fall kinematics and use this velocity in the change in momentum equation.","Yes, calculate the speed from energy conservation and use this speed in the change in momentum equation."]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of energy while on the frictionless hill can give the speed at the top of the 10-m hill:</p> a_c = \omega^2 r = (2\pi f)^2 r $$
<p class="numpt">Then impulse equals change in momentum can be used since the final momentum must be zero.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q20', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 20, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An object of mass $m$ starts at a height of $H_{1}$ with a speed of $v_{1}$. A few minutes later, it is at a height of $H_{2}$ and a speed $v_{2}$. Which of the following expressions best represents the work done to the mass by nongravitational forces to the object during this time?</p>', '["(mg(H_2 - H_1) + \frac{1}{2}m(v_2^2 - v_1^2))","(mg(H_2 - H_1) - \frac{1}{2}m(v_2^2 - v_1^2))","(\frac{1}{2}m(v_2^2 - v_1^2))","(\frac{1}{2}m(v_1^2 - v_2^2))"]'::jsonb, '$$ L = I\omega \implies I_1\omega_1 = I_2\omega_2 $$
<p class="numpt">Work by forces other than gravity = change in energy</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q21', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 21, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An object with 0.2-kg mass is pushed down vertically onto an elastic spring ($k$ = 20 N/m). The spring is compressed by 20 cm and then released such that the object will fly off. Which of the following will have the largest effect on increasing the maximum height the object will fly? (Assume no air resistance.)</p>', '["Halving the mass","Doubling the compression distance","Using a spring with a spring constant twice as big","Doing the same experiment on a different planet with half the gravitational field strength"]'::jsonb, 'Energy conservation:</p> \omega_2 = \frac{I_1}{I_1/3}\omega_1 = 3\omega_1 $$
<p class="numpt">Solving for $h$:</p> KE_{\text{rot}} = \frac{1}{2}I\omega^2 = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{I_1}{3}\right)(3\omega_1)^2 = 3\left(\frac{1}{2}I_1\omega_1^2\right)  
<p class="numpt">Doubling $x$ will quadruple the height, whereas all other factors will only double the height.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q22', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 22, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A cart with a mass of $m$ needs to complete a loop-the-loop of radius $r$, as shown above. What is the approximate minimum velocity required to achieve this goal?</p>', '["(<i>gr</i>)<sup>½</sup>","(5<i>gr</i>)<sup>½</sup>","2(<i>gr</i>)<sup>½</sup>","(2<i>gr</i>)<sup>½</sup>"]'::jsonb, 'Circular motion at the top:</p> \alpha = \frac{\omega_f - \omega_i}{t} = \frac{0 - 150(2\pi/60)}{2} = \frac{-5\pi}{2} = -2.5\pi\text{ rad/s}^2 $$
<p class="numpt">The lowest speed will be when there is no normal force ($N$ = 0):</p>
<p class="center1">$mg = m v^2/r, v_{\text{top}} = (gr)^{1/2}$</p>
<p class="numpt">Kinetic energy at the bottom must give both this speed and potential energy to gain 2$r$ in height:</p>
<p class="center1">$E_{\text{bottom}} = E_{\text{top}}, \frac{1}{2}m v^2 = g m (2r) + \frac{1}{2} m v_{\text{top}}^2$</p>
<p class="numpt">Solving for $v$:</p>
<p class="center1">$v = (5gr)^{1/2}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q23', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 23, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A particle traveling in the direction shown below experiences the force shown. In this situation, the particle will</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w67" role="presentation" src="images/f183-02.jpg"/></p>', '["slow down only","speed up only","turn and speed up","neither turn nor change speed"]'::jsonb, 'Since the force shown has components both in the same direction as the velocity (causing it to speed up) and at right angles to the velocity (causing it to turn toward the bottom of the page), the particle will both speed up and turn.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q24', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 24, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following velocity component versus time graphs match a person riding an elevator steadily downward on a cruise boat that is accelerating out of port (assume $y$ is vertical and $x$ is horizontal)?</p>', '["<img alt=\"\" class=\"w58 inlinet\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f183-04.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w58 inlinet\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f183-05.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w58 inlinet\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f183-06.jpg\"/>","<img alt=\"\" class=\"w58 inlinet\" role=\"presentation\" src=\"images/f183-07.jpg\"/>"]'::jsonb, 'Since the elevator is moving downward at constant speed, the $y$-component of velocity should be a constant negative value. On the other hand, the horizontal acceleration of the boat will cause an increasingly positive $x$-component of velocity.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q25', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 25, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'If a mass of 9 kg begins at rest and experiences the following net force, what is its speed after 3 seconds?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w67" role="presentation" src="images/f183-08.jpg"/></p>', '["0 m/s","1 m/s","2 m/s","4 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse is the area under the curve. For the first three seconds, this amounts to</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{2}(6)(3) = 9\text{ N}\cdot\text{s}$</p>
<p class="numpt">This impulse will increase the momentum by 9 kg m/s. Since the object has a mass of 9 kg, this indicates an increase in speed of 1 m/s.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q26', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 26, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Two blocks are sliding to the right as shown below. If they are brought to a stop by a force $F$ applied from the right, what is the net work done to the two blocks as they are brought to a stop?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w25" role="presentation" src="images/f184-01.jpg"/></p>', '["−1.5 <i>mv</i><sup>2</sup>","−0.5 <i>mv</i><sup>2</sup>","−<i>Fv</i>","−<i>mv</i><sup>2</sup>"]'::jsonb, 'Work done is equal to the change in kinetic energy:</p>
<p class="center1">$K_f - K_i = 0 - \frac{1}{2}(3m)v^2 = -1.5 m v^2$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q27', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 27, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A satellite is launched to twice the orbital radius of a similar satellite (both are in stable circular Earth orbits). Compare the kinetic energies of the two satellites.</p>', '["They have the same kinetic energy.","The lower satellite has half the kinetic energy of the higher satellite.","The higher satellite has half the kinetic energy of the lower satellite.","The ratio of the kinetic energies of the two satellites is a factor of four."]'::jsonb, 'Circular orbits have universal gravity acting as the centripetal force, which causes the centripetal acceleration of $v^{2}$/$r$:</p>
<p class="center1">$F_c = m a_c, \frac{G M_e M}{r^2} = \frac{m v^2}{r}$</p></li>
<li><p class="numpt">Since all other values are constant, we see here $v^{2}$ is proportional to 1/$r$.</p></li>
<li><p class="nump">So an orbit that has twice the radius will have half the $v^{2}$.</p></li>
<li><p class="nump">Kinetic energy varies a $v^{2}$:</p>
<p class="center1">$K = \frac{1}{2} m v^2$</p>
<p class="numpt">Thus, the higher orbital will have half the kinetic energy of the lower orbit.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q28', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 28, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'It has been documented that a major earthquake on Earth can change the time it takes the planet to rotate fully. From this, one can conclude that</p>', '["the Earth is not an isolated system","a net torque was applied to the planet","the rotational inertia of the planet was changed","work was done to the planet somehow"]'::jsonb, 'A change in rotational speed can either be accomplished via an interaction with an external torque or by a change in rotational inertia, which keeps angular momentum conserved in an isolated system by adjusting the angular speed:</p>
<p class="center1">$I\omega = \text{constant}$</p>
<p class="numpt">Since the earthquake is not an external force to the Earth, the shifting of mass to different radial values must have changed the rotational inertia of the planet. This is similar to a diver adjusting their spin rate by extending or tucking their body in as they drop into the water.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q29', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 29, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Compare the two columns of water pictured below. They have the same height. Which of the following statements compares the total weight and the pressure at the bottom of the columns?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w38" role="presentation" src="images/f184-02.jpg"/></p>', '["Weight and pressure are greater for the wider column.","Weight and pressure are the same for both.","Weight is greater for the wider column, while pressure is the same.","Weight is the same, while pressure is greater for the wider column."]'::jsonb, 'Weight is greater for the wider column, while pressure is the same. Although the wider column has a greater volume of water and therefore more mass, the fact that the height remains constant means any unit area on the bottom of the column will experience the same force/area (pressure).</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q30', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 30, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An otherwise similar boat will ride higher in a fluid (i.e., have less submerged volume) if</p>', '["the fluid’s density is raised","the fluid’s temperature is raised","the boat’s density is raised","the boat shifts its cargo to a lower deck"]'::jsonb, 'The fluid’s density is raised. The buoyancy force that lifts the boat upward is proportional to the weight of the fluid displaced. The boat will rise or sink until the buoyant force and the boat’s weight cancel. Raising the fluid’s temperature will usually make the fluid less dense, requiring a greater displacement of water (i.e., the boat will ride lower). Raising the boat’s density will increase its weight, requiring a greater displacement of water. Finally, shifting the cargo around will have no effect as the weight and shape of the boat remain unaffected.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q31', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 31, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'Fluid is poured into an open U-shaped tube as shown below. If a person blows across the top of the right-hand side’s opening, what will happen to the fluid in the tube?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w38" role="presentation" src="images/f184-03.jpg"/></p>', '["It will remain the same.","It will rise on the right-hand side and lower on the left-hand side.","It will rise on both sides.","It will rise on the left-hand side and lower on the right-hand side."]'::jsonb, 'It will rise on the right-hand side and lower on the left-hand side. Blowing over the right-hand side will lower the pressure at that height:</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho gh + P = \text{constant}$</p>
<p class="numpt">Remember that $h$ remains the same while $v$ is raised, forcing $P$ lower. This causes an imbalance in pressure between the right-hand side (lower pressure) and left-hand side (higher pressure). This difference in pressure causes a net force pushing the fluid upward on the right and downward on the left.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q32', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 32, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A boat moving due north crosses a river 240 meters wide with a velocity of 8 meters per second relative to the water. The river flows east with a velocity of 6 meters per second. How far downstream will the boat be when it has crossed the river?</p>', '["240 m","180 m","420 m","300 m"]'::jsonb, 'Find time to cross the river using only perpendicular components:</p>
<p class="center1">$D = V_y t, 240 = 8t, t = 30\text{ s}$</p>
<p class="numpt">Next find the downstream distance using the parallel component:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w37" role="presentation" src="images/f193-08.jpg"/></p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q33', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 33, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A variable force acts on a 2-kilogram mass according to the graph below.</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w67" role="presentation" src="images/f185-01.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">How much work was done while displacing the mass 10 meters?</p>', '["40 J","38 J","32 J","30 J"]'::jsonb, 'Work is the area under the curve:</p>
<p class="center1">$(4\text{ N})(6\text{ m}) + (2\text{ N})(4\text{ m}) = 24\text{ J} + 8\text{ J} = 32\text{ J}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q34', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 34, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A man weighing himself is standing on a bathroom scale in an elevator that is accelerating upward at a rate of 0.5 meter per second squared. By what percentage is the reading of the scale off from the person’s true weight?</p>', '["0% (accurate)","5% too high","5% too low","0.5% too high"]'::jsonb, 'The bathroom scale reads the normal force:</p>
<p class="center1">$F_{\text{net}} = N - mg = ma, 0.5\text{ m/s}^2 = 0.05g, N - mg = m(0.05g), N = m(1.05g)$</p>
<p class="numpt">The reading will be 5% too high.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q35', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 35, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'A 50-kilogram person is sitting on a seesaw 1.2 meters from the balance point. On the other side, a 70-kilogram person is balanced. How far from the balance point is the second person sitting?</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w70" role="presentation" src="images/f185-02.jpg"/></p>', '["0.57 m","0.75 m","0.63 m","0.86 m"]'::jsonb, 'Balanced means the torques are equal and opposite:</p>
<p class="center1">\$50\text{ kg} \cdot g \cdot 1.2 = 70\text{ kg} \cdot g \cdot x, x = 6/7 = 0.86\text{ m}$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q36', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 36, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'An object rolls down a steep incline with very little friction. At the same time, an object of equal mass slides down a similar but frictionless incline. Which one takes less time to get to the bottom?</p>', '["They take the same time.","The rolling object takes less time.","The sliding object takes less time.","The answer depends on the rolling object’s rotational inertia."]'::jsonb, 'Both objects start with the same potential energy. However, the rolling object must use some of that potential energy for rotational energy, leaving less for linear kinetic energy. Therefore, the rolling object moves more slowly down the hill.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q37', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 37, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'What is the value of $g$ at a position above Earth’s surface equal to Earth’s radius?</p>', '["9.8 N/kg","4.9 N/kg","2.45 N/kg","1.6 N/kg"]'::jsonb, 'Universal gravity (and the gravitational field) are 1/$r^{2}$ laws; doubling $r$ will quarter the field.</p>
<p class="center1">$\frac{1}{4}g = \frac{1}{4}(9.8\text{ N/kg})$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q38', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 38, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If an object is spinning at 150 RPM (revolutions per minute) and comes to a stop in 2 seconds, what is its average acceleration in radians/$s^{2}$?</p>', '["−2.5π","−2π","−5π","−10π"]'::jsonb, 'First, convert RPMs to rad/s:</p>
<p class="center1">\$150\text{ rev/min} \times (2\pi/1\text{ rev}) \times (1\text{ min}/60\text{ s}) = 5\pi\text{ rad/s}, \text{acceleration} = \frac{0 - 5\pi\text{ rad/s}}{2\text{ s}} = -2.5\pi\text{ rad/s}^2$</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q39', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 39, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'What is the work done by a horizontal spring (spring constant $k$) expanding from a compression distance $x$ to an extension distance $x$ to an attached mass?</p>', '["2<i>kx</i><sup>2</sup>","½<i>kx</i><sup>2</sup>","<i>kx</i><sup>2</sup>","0"]'::jsonb, '$ \omega $ of work is delivered to the mass while uncompressing, followed by − $ \omega $ done while the mass extends the spring outward, totaling to 0 net work for the entire expansion. Alternatively, think about the velocity being zero at the beginning and end of that single oscillation. No change in kinetic energy occurs; therefore, no net work is done.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q40', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 40, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If an isolated spinning object’s rotational inertia is reduced by a factor of 3 by internal forces, how will its angular momentum change?</p>', '["Angular momentum will be 3 times its previous value.","Angular momentum will be reduced to 1/3 its previous value.","Angular momentum will be reduced to 1/9 its previous value.","Angular momentum will remain unchanged."]'::jsonb, 'No external torque means momentum must be conserved.</p></li>
</ol>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q41', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 41, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A mass is suspended from the ceiling by one rope at an angle as indicated. The mass is held in place by a second, horizontal rope. Without knowing the exact angle from the ceiling, except that it is greater than 45 degrees, which statement best represents the possible value for tension in the horizontal line?</p>', '["$T$ &gt; <i>mg</i>","$T$ = <i>mg</i>","$T$ &lt; <i>mg</i>","Cannot be determined without knowing the angle"]'::jsonb, '$T$ &lt; <i>mg.</i> Since the mass is stationary, we conclude that the net force is zero. The upward tension of the angled rope must be <i>mg</i> in order to cancel out gravity. The free-body diagram on the hanging mass is:</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w35" role="presentation" src="images/f023-01.jpg"/></p>
<p class="exp">Since the angle θ is greater than 45 degrees, the vertical component of tension $T_{2}$ must be bigger than the horizontal component. (To see this quickly, draw an extreme case, such as 85 degrees.) Therefore, the horizontal component of tension must be less than <i>mg</i>. The horizontal rope $T$ must cancel this horizontal component of $T_{2}$.</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w23" role="presentation" src="images/f023-02.jpg"/></p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q42', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 42, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Pushing on a mass of 15 kg with a sideways force of 120 N is not enough to get the mass to start sliding across the floor. Which of the following best describes the coefficient of static friction between the floor and the mass?</p>', '["$\\mu$ = 0.8","$\\mu$ &gt; 0.8","$\\mu$ &lt; 0.8","$\\mu$ = 1.25"]'::jsonb, '$\mu$ &gt; 0.8. The normal force between the mass and the floor is 150 N. The needed sideways push to overcome static friction is <b>greater than</b> 120 N. Dividing these two forces would give us:</p>
<p class="center1">$\mu_s = 120/150 = 0.8$</p>
<p class="exp">Since a greater force is needed, the coefficient of static friction must be greater than this value.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q43', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 43, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'While standing on an elevator and experiencing a downward acceleration of 4.9 m/$s^{2}$, what is the force between the floor and your feet? The variable $m$ is your mass.</p>', '["<i>mg</i>","0.5<i>mg</i>","1.5<i>mg</i>","4.9<i>mg</i>"]'::jsonb, '0.5<i>mg</i>. The free-body diagram consists of only two forces.</p> N - mg = ma $$
<p class="exp">Newton’s second law gives us $+N$ − <i>mg</i> = <i>ma</i>. Let $a$ = −0.5$g$, which is half the value of $g$ and downward:</p> N - mg = m(-0.5g) \implies N = 0.5mg $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q44', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 44, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'After throwing a ball at an upward angle, the reason the ball continues to go horizontally while falling due to gravity is that</p>', '["the ball’s inertia keeps it going","the force of the throw keeps it going","the energy from the gravitational potential keeps replenishing the lost energy","air pressure keeps the ball from falling too quickly"]'::jsonb, 'The ball’s inertia keeps it going. Newton’s first law says that a body’s inertia maintains its current motion once in motion. After the force of the throw, the only force on the ball is the vertical force of gravity. This causes the ball to accelerate in the vertical direction while the ball’s sideways velocity is maintained due to its inertia.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-1_q45', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-1', 45, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Consider the situation in which your hand is pushing a book across a rough desktop with lots of friction. Of the many forces involved, consider only these two individual forces: the force on your hand from the book and the force the book is experiencing from your hand. While the book is accelerating from rest to its final velocity, which statement best compares the force experienced by your hand compared with that experienced by the book?</p>', '["Force on book &gt; force on hand","Force on hand &gt; force on book","Force on hand = force on book","The relationship between these two forces depends on the frictional force."]'::jsonb, 'Force on hand = force on book. Newton’s third law of action-reaction states that the force pair of interaction between two objects is always equal and opposite. The acceleration of either object has no role in the third law. The acceleration comes about from the net force on a single object.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q1', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 1, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A block ($m$ = 1.5 kg) is pushed along a frictionless surface for a distance of 2.5 meters, as shown above. How much work has been done if a force of 10 newtons makes an angle of 60 degrees with the horizontal?</p>', '["Zero","12.5 J","21.6 J","25 J"]'::jsonb, 'Work = <i>fd</i> cos 60° = (10 N)(2.5)(0.5) = 12.5 J</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q2', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 2, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'What is the instantaneous power due to the gravitational force acting on a 3-kilogram projectile the instant the projectile is traveling with a velocity of 10 meters per second at an angle of 30 degrees above the horizontal?</p>', '["300 W","150 W","−150 W","−300 W"]'::jsonb, '$$ W = F d \cos(60^\circ) = (10\text{ N})(2.5\text{ m})(0.5) = 12.5\text{ J} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q3', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 3, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A hockey puck of an unknown mass is sliding along ice that can be considered frictionless with a velocity of 10 meters per second. The puck then crosses over onto a rough floor that has a coefficient of kinetic friction equal to 0.2. How far will the puck travel before friction stops it?</p>', '["2.5 m","5 m","25 m","Depends on the mass"]'::jsonb, '$$ P = F v = (50\text{ N})(10\text{ m/s}) = 500\text{ W} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q4', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 4, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A dart is placed onto a spring. The spring is stretched a distance $x$. By what factor must the spring’s elongation be changed so that the maximum kinetic energy given to the dart is doubled?</p>', '["$\\frac{1}{2}$","2","4","$\\sqrt{2}$"]'::jsonb, 'Conservation of energy:</p> PE_s = \frac{1}{2}kx^2 \implies \text{doubling } PE \implies x_{\text{new}} = \sqrt{2}\,x $$
<p class="exp">Since we need $x^{2}$ to double, increase $x$ by (2)<sup>½</sup> = \sqrt{2}.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q5', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 5, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A 10-kilogram projectile is launched at a 60° angle to the ground, with a velocity of 200 m/s. Neglect air resistance. The ground is level.</p>
<p class="exp">Compare this projectile with a 5-kilogram projectile launched under the same conditions but at a 30° angle. The 5-kilogram projectile will</p>', '["go higher up and farther along the ground","go equally high and equally far along the ground","neither go as high nor as far along the ground","not go as high but go equally far along the ground"]'::jsonb, 'The 30° and 60° angles will have equal ranges but with the roles of $V$<sub>$x$</sub> and $V$<sub>$y$</sub> reversed. Therefore, the projectile launched at a 30° angle will not go as high. Note that mass does not enter into projectile motion problems.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q6', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 6, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A uniform rod of mass $M$ and length $L$ is rotated about its center as shown below. Given that the rotational inertia of a rod about its center is (1/12)<i>ML</i><sup>2</sup>, determine the net torque experience by the rod as it rotates from an angular speed of $w_{1}$ to $w_{2}$ in 0.5 seconds.</p>
<p class="center1"><img alt="" class="w31" role="presentation" src="images/f204-01.jpg"/></p>', '["((omega_2 - omega_1) M L^2 / 12)","((omega_2 - omega_1) M L^2 / 6)","(omega_2 omega_1 M L^2)","((omega_2 / omega_1) M L^2 / 12)"]'::jsonb, 'First determine an expression for the angular acceleration:</p> \alpha = \frac{\Delta\omega}{\Delta t} = \frac{20 - 0}{4} = 5\text{ rad/s}^2 $$
<p class="exp">Now use this in the expression for net torque:</p> \tau = I\alpha = (2\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m}^2)(5\text{ rad/s}^2) = 10\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}  
<p class="exp">Finally, substitute in the given expression for the rotational inertia:</p> \theta = \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2 = \frac{1}{2}(5)(4^2) = 40\text{ rad} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q7', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 7, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'An object of mass $m$ rests on top of a spring that has been compressed by $x$ meters. The force constant for this spring is <i>k.</i> The mass is not attached to the spring and will shoot upward when the spring is uncompressed. When released, how high will the mass rise?</p>', '["<i>mg</i> − <i>kx</i>","<i>kx</i><sup>2</sup>/<i>mg</i>","(<i>kx</i><sup>2</sup>/2<i>mg</i>) − $x$","($k$/$m$)<sup>1/2</sup> $x$"]'::jsonb, '$$ a_c = \frac{v^2}{r} = \frac{10^2}{5} = 20\text{ m/s}^2  
<p class="exp">$h$ = <i>kx</i><sup><i>2</i></sup>/2<i>mg</i> above the starting point. Since the release point is a distance $x$ above the starting point, we must subtract $x$ from the answer.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q8', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 8, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'Which of the following is the best method for finding a spring’s force constant $k$?</p>', '["Hanging a known mass on the spring and dividing the weight by the length of the spring","Hanging several known masses on the spring, taking the average value of the mass, and dividing by the average length of the spring","Hanging several known masses on the spring and finding the area under the curve after plotting force versus extension","Hanging several known masses on the spring and finding the slope of the graph after plotting force versus extension"]'::jsonb, '$F$ = <i>kx</i>. So when graphing $f$ versus $x$, $k$ will be the slope. The extension of the spring is $x$ when $F$ is the force applied to the spring.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q9', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 9, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'How much of a braking force is applied to a 2,500-kilogram car on the Moon ($g$ = 1.6 m/$s^{2}$) that has an initial velocity of 30 meters per second if the car is brought to a stop in 15 seconds?</p>', '["5,000 N","6,000 N","8,000 N","25,000 N"]'::jsonb, '$$ F_{\text{brake}} = ma = (2500\text{ kg})\left(\frac{30\text{ m/s}}{15\text{ s}}\right) = 5000\text{ N}  
<p class="exp">The negative sign indicates an opposing force. Note that weight is not needed in this calculation, so the value of $g$ is irrelevant.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q10', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 10, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'easy', 'A 1-kilogram object is moving to the right with a velocity of 6 meters per second. It collides with and sticks to a 2-kilogram mass, which is also moving to the right, with a velocity of 3 meters per second. What happens to the total kinetic energy during this collision?</p>', '["The kinetic energy is conserved because the collision is elastic.","The kinetic energy is conserved even though the collision is not elastic.","Some kinetic energy is lost during the collision even though total momentum is conserved.","Some kinetic energy is lost during the collision because of the elastic nature of the collision."]'::jsonb, 'Momentum is always conserved. However, kinetic energy is lost unless the collision is elastic, in which case the kinetic energy is also conserved:</p> p_i = (1)(6) + (2)(0) = 6\text{ kg}\cdot\text{m/s} $$
<p class="exp">Initial <i>KE</i>:</p> KE_i = \frac{1}{2}(1)(6^2) = 18\text{ J}, \quad KE_f = \frac{1}{2}(1)(0) + \frac{1}{2}(2)(3.87^2) = 15\text{ J}  
<p class="exp">Final <i>KE</i>:</p> \Delta KE = 18\text{ J} - 15\text{ J} = 3\text{ J (lost)} $$
<p class="exp">3 joules are lost.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q11', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 11, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A ball with a mass of 0.2 kilogram strikes a wall with a velocity of 3 meters per second. It bounces straight back with a velocity of 1 meter per second. What was the magnitude of the impulse delivered to this ball?</p>', '["0.2 kg · m/s","0.4 kg · m/s","0.6 kg · m/s","0.8 kg · m/s"]'::jsonb, 'Impulse = Δ$p = p$<sub>$f$</sub> − $p$<sub>$i$</sub> = 0.2(−1) − (0.2)(3) = −0.8 kg · m/s</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q12', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 12, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following is an equivalent expression for the maximum velocity attained by a mass $m$ oscillating horizontally along a frictionless surface? The mass is attached to a spring with a force constant $k$ and has an amplitude of $A$.</p>', '["<i>Ak</i>/$m$","$A$($k$/$m$)½","<i>mg</i>/<i>kA</i>","$A^{2} k$/$m$"]'::jsonb, '$$ mgh = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \implies v = \sqrt{2gh}  
<p class="exp">Conservation of energy:</p> \frac{v_A}{v_B} = \sqrt{\frac{h_A}{h_B}} = \sqrt{\frac{4}{1}} = 2 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q13', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 13, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A 0.5-kilogram mass is attached to a spring with a force constant of 50 newtons per meter. What is the total energy stored in the mass-spring system if the mass travels a distance of 8 cm in one cycle?</p>', '["0.5 J","0.01 J","0.04 J","0.08 J"]'::jsonb, 'In one cycle, the mass travels 4 amplitudes:</p> a = \frac{F - f_k}{m} = \frac{50 - 10}{10} = 4\text{ m/s}^2 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q14', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 14, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following expressions is equivalent to the magnitude of the escape velocity in terms of the magnitude of the orbital velocity $v$ for a spacecraft?</p>', '["2$v$","$v$","4$v$","$\\sqrt{2}v$"]'::jsonb, 'Orbital velocity:</p> v_{\text{orbit}} = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{R}} $$
<p class="exp">Escape velocity:</p> v_{\text{escape}} = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}} = \sqrt{2}\,v_{\text{orbit}} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q15', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 15, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following graphs correctly shows the relationship between magnitude of gravitational force and distance between two masses?</p>', '["$ 10\\text{ N} $","$ 20\\text{ N} $","$ 30\\text{ N} $","$ 40\\text{ N} $"]'::jsonb, 'The gravitational force between two masses has a 1/$r^{2}$ relationship (where $r$ is the distance from center to center). Graph B shows the correct inverse relationship.</p></li>
<li>')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.ap_exam_questions (id, test_id, question_order, is_frq, answer_key, topic, difficulty, question_text, options_json, explanation)
VALUES
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q16', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 16, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements is true regarding why the net work done in lifting a book from the ground to the top of a shelf is zero?</p>', '["Air resistance (friction) took away any increase in kinetic energy.","Gravitational potential energies are always negative.","The force used to lift the book is a conservative force.","The change in kinetic energy of the book is zero."]'::jsonb, 'Net work done to an object results in a change in kinetic energy. In this case the book has no kinetic energy at the beginning and at the end of its trip:</p> a_c = \omega^2 R $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q17', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 17, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following best describes the forces present as a brick is sliding along the horizontal ground and coming to a halt?</p>', '["The net force is equal to the force of friction.","The inertia of the brick is supplied by the original push that got the brick moving.","The forces on the brick are in equilibrium.","The brick is pushing on the air in front of it harder than the air is pushing back on the brick."]'::jsonb, 'The brick is experiencing three forces: a vertical normal and weight force that cancel out as well as a horizontal force of friction opposing the motion. This force is unopposed and is the cause of the deceleration of the brick. The inertia of the brick is provided by the fact that it is a mass in motion and would continue to move at constant velocity if there were no friction present. The pushing force is no longer active on the brick and plays no role in the analysis.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q18', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 18, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A projectile is launched at a 30° angle to the ground with a velocity of 200 m/s. What is its speed at its maximum height?</p>', '["9.8 m/s","100 m/s","173 m/s","200 m/s"]'::jsonb, 'At maximum height, all speed is from $V$<sub>$x$</sub>:</p> T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} \implies g = L\left(\frac{2\pi}{T}\right)^2 $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q19', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 19, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A conical pendulum consists of a mass $m$ attached to a light string of length $L$. The mass swings around in a horizontal circle, making an angle θ with the vertical as shown above. What is the magnitude of tension, $T$, in the string?</p>', '["<i>mg</i>/cos θ","<i>mg</i> cos θ","<i>mg</i>/sin θ","<i>mg</i> sin θ"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical forces must cancel:</p> F = -kx \implies k = \frac{F}{x} = \frac{40\text{ N}}{0.1\text{ m}} = 400\text{ N/m} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q20', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 20, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'The magnitude of the one-dimensional momentum of a 2-kilogram particle obeys the relationship $p$ = 2$t$ + 3. What was the velocity of the particle at $t$ = 1 second?</p>', '["5 m/s","2 m/s","1 m/s","2.5 m/s"]'::jsonb, '$v = p$/$m$ = (2 × 1 + 3)/2 = 2.5 m/s</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q21', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 21, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An object is experiencing a nonzero net force. Which of the following statements is most accurate?</p>', '["The linear and angular momentums of the object are both definitely changing.","Although the linear momentum of the object is definitely changing, the angular momentum may not be.","Although the angular momentum of the object is definitely changing, the linear momentum may not be.","Neither the linear momentum nor the angular momentum is definitely changing."]'::jsonb, 'Since $ \vec{F}_{\text{net}} = \frac{\Delta\vec{p}}{\Delta t} $, a net force will change the linear momentum. However, torque also involves the lever arm (τ = <i>RF</i> sin θ). So despite having a nonzero net force, the net torque might still be zero.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q22', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 22, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'A car with a 500-newton driver goes over a hill that has a radius of 50 meters as shown above. The velocity of the car is 20 meters per second. What are the approximate force and the direction that the car exerts on the driver?</p>', '["900 N, up","400 N, down","100 N, up","500 N, up"]'::jsonb, 'Circular motion:</p> N - mg = m\left(\frac{v^2}{R}\right) $$
<p class="exp">$N$ is the force the car exerts on the driver:</p> N = m\left(g + \frac{v^2}{R}\right) $$
<p class="exp">Note that the correct answer can be found by simply knowing that the net force must be down and that the car must be pushing upward on the passenger.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q23', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 23, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'What is the net torque acting on the pivot supporting a uniform stationary 10-kilogram beam 2 meters long as shown above?</p>', '["198 N · m","−198 N · m","−102 N · m","102 N · m"]'::jsonb, 'Taking the pivot as the reference point, the weight of the beam (mg = 10 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 98 N) acts at the center of mass, 1.0 meter from the pivot. The stationary beam is in static equilibrium, so the net torque acting on the system is zero (τ_net = 0 N·m).'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q24', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 24, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'Which of the following statements best describes the acceleration over the 55 seconds of time graphed above?</p>', '["Increasing, constant, decreasing, increasing","Positive, zero, negative, positive","Positive until the 30 second mark, then negative for the rest of the trip","The net acceleration is approximately 500 m/s/s for the entire trip."]'::jsonb, 'Acceleration is the slope on velocity versus time graphs. Since there are four separate intervals of straight lines, there are four intervals of constant acceleration: positive for the first 10 seconds, zero for the subsequent 5 seconds, a negative acceleration from the 15th to the 40th second, and positive for the last 15 seconds.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q25', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 25, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'What is the average acceleration for the 55 seconds of the graph?</p>', '["Approximately 500/55 m/s/s","Approximately 5/4 m/s/s","Approximately −100/25 m/s/s","Approximately 0 m/s/s"]'::jsonb, 'To find the average acceleration for any interval on a velocity vs. time graph, connect the beginning of the interval to the end of the interval and find the slope of that line. In this case, the graph begins and ends on the axis and therefore the connecting line is horizontal: slope = 0.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q26', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 26, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'How far does the car travel over the 55 seconds of the graph?</p>', '["1,550 m","550 m","0 m","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, 'Displacement is area under the curve: +1,050 - 500 = 550 m</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q27', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 27, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'What is the instantaneous acceleration at the 40 second mark?</p>', '["0 m/s/s","−4/3 m/s/s","−10/3 m/s/s","−4 m/s/s"]'::jsonb, 'What is the instantaneous acceleration is the slope at that point: −100/25 = −4 m/s/s</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q28', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 28, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'An old record player could bring a disk up to its 45 RPM speed in less than a second. If the same size disk can also be brought up to a speed of 75 RPM in about the same amount of time on another player, compare the two torques.</p>', '["The torques would be the same as the rotational inertia of the two disks is the same.","The torques would be the same because of the conservation of angular momentum.","The torque would be larger in the second case as it requires a greater angular acceleration.","The torque would be larger in the second case as it entails both a larger force and a larger lever arm."]'::jsonb, '$$ \alpha = \frac{\tau}{I} = \frac{F r}{I} $$
<p class="exp">Our only data provided are that the angular acceleration must be larger for the 75 RPM record and that the rotational inertia has not changed (same disk). Therefore, the torque supplied must be larger.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q29', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 29, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'How does the speed of the vertically launched sphere compare to the speed of the horizontally launched sphere as they each hit the floor?</p>', '["It is the same.","It is twice as great.","It is greater but not necessarily twice as great.","It is less."]'::jsonb, 'Remember conservation of energy. Since both start at the same height (same potential energy) with the same kinetic energy, they will both hit the ground with the same joules of energy (all kinetic energy). The same kinetic energy means the same speed since their masses are the same. Note that this does not imply that both components of velocity are the same. They are not. This implies that only the magnitudes of the final velocity vectors are the same.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q30', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 30, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'medium', 'How does the time required for the vertically projected sphere to hit the floor compare with that for the horizontally projected sphere?</p>', '["It is the same.","It is twice as great.","It is greater but not necessarily twice as great.","It is less."]'::jsonb, 'The vertically projected sphere will spend much more time in the air as it goes much higher. Without knowing the exact speed of the launch, it is not possible to say by what factor the time in flight is extended.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q31', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 31, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'How does the work done by gravity to the vertically launched sphere compare to the work done by gravity to the horizontally launched sphere?</p>', '["It is the same.","It is twice as great.","It is greater but not necessarily twice as great.","It is less."]'::jsonb, 'The change in gravitational potential energy is the same for both. Therefore, the work done by gravity is the same.</p></li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q32', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 32, false, 'C', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If the density of the liquid is $\rho$<sub>$L$</sub> and the density of the cube if $\rho$<sub>$c$</sub>, which of the following is the correction expression of the mass of the cube? The cube is half submerged in the liquid.</p>', '["$ m $","$ 2m $","$ 3m $","$ 4m $"]'::jsonb, 'First use the definition of density to find an expression for the mass of the cube:</p> T_1 = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} $$
<p class="exp">Since this is not one of the answers, find an expression for equilibrium using Archimedes’ principle of buoyancy:</p> T_2 = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m + \Delta m}{k}} = 2 T_1 \implies m + \Delta m = 4m  
<p class="exp">Solve for mass:</p> \Delta m = 3m $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q33', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 33, false, 'D', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'If the cube is submerged just under the surface of the liquid and then released, what will be the initial upward acceleration experienced by the cube?</p>', '["$ a = 0 $","$ a = g/3 $","$ a = g/2 $","$ a = g $"]'::jsonb, 'The net force is the difference between the buoyancy force and the weight of the cube:</p> F_{\text{net}} = mg - T = ma $$
<p class="exp">Since this is the net force, to find the resulting acceleration, divide by the mass of the cube:</p> a = \frac{mg - T}{m} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q34', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 34, false, 'B', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'How many radians of rotation did the disk experience during these 3 seconds?</p>', '["60","55","45","15"]'::jsonb, 'The angular displacement is the area under the given graph:</p> \rho_{\text{obj}} V_{\text{obj}} g = \rho_{\text{fluid}} V_{\text{sub}} g \implies \frac{V_{\text{sub}}}{V_{\text{obj}}} = \frac{\rho_{\text{obj}}}{\rho_{\text{fluid}}} $$</li>
<li>'),
  ('ap-mock-phys1-full-2_q35', 'ap-mock-phys1-full-2', 35, false, 'A', 'Comprehensive AP Physics 1', 'hard', 'What is the change in angular momentum experienced by the disk during each of the 3 seconds of rotation?</p>
<p><b>Columns:</b> 0–1 seconds &bull; 1–2 seconds &bull; 2–3 seconds</p>', '["0 | 0 | -10 I","20 I | 20 I | -15 I","0 | 0 | -10 I /3","-10 I /3 | -10 I /3 | -10 I /3"]'::jsonb, 'The change in angular momentum is the torque multiplied by the time:</p> \Delta L = \tau \Delta t $$
<p class="exp">Since each time interval is only 1 second:</p> \tau = I\alpha \implies \Delta L = I\alpha \Delta t  
<p class="exp">where $\alpha$ is the slope of the line in each interval.</p>
<div class="top">
